<p id="mwlg"><b id="mwlw">Operation Overlord</b> was the codename for the <b id="mwmA">Battle of Normandy</b>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allies_of_World_War_II" title="Allies of World War II" id="mwmQ">Allied</a> operation that launched the successful liberation of German-occupied <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Front_(World_War_II)" title="Western Front (World War II)" id="mwmg">Western Europe</a> during <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II" title="World War II" id="mwmw">World War II</a>. The operation was launched on 6 June 1944 (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-Day_(military_term)" title="D-Day (military term)" id="mwnA">D-Day</a>) with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_landings" title="Normandy landings" id="mwnQ">Normandy landings</a> (Operation Neptune). A 1,200-plane <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airborne_forces" title="Airborne forces" id="mwng">airborne assault</a> preceded an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphibious_warfare" title="Amphibious warfare" id="mwnw">amphibious assault</a> involving more than 5,000 vessels. Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Channel" title="English Channel" id="mwoA">English Channel</a> on 6 June, and more than two million Allied troops were in France by the end of August.</p>

<p id="mwoQ">The decision to undertake cross-channel landings in 1944 was made at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Conference_(1943)" title="Washington Conference (1943)" id="mwog">Trident Conference</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C." title="Washington, D.C." id="mwow">Washington</a> in May 1943. American General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwight_D._Eisenhower" title="Dwight D. Eisenhower" id="mwpA">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> was appointed commander of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Headquarters_Allied_Expeditionary_Force" title="Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force" id="mwpQ">Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force</a>, and British General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Montgomery" title="Bernard Montgomery" id="mwpg">Bernard Montgomery</a> was named commander of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/21st_Army_Group" title="21st Army Group" id="mwpw">21st Army Group</a>, which comprised all the land forces involved in the operation. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy" title="Normandy" id="mwqA">Normandy</a> coast in northwestern France was chosen as the site of the landings, with the Americans assigned to land at sectors codenamed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah_Beach" title="Utah Beach" id="mwqQ">Utah</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omaha_Beach" title="Omaha Beach" id="mwqg">Omaha</a>, the British at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sword_Beach" title="Sword Beach" id="mwqw">Sword</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Beach" title="Gold Beach" id="mwrA">Gold</a>, and the Canadians at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juno_Beach" title="Juno Beach" id="mwrQ">Juno</a>. To meet the conditions expected on the Normandy beachhead, special technology was developed, including two artificial ports called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_harbours" title="Mulberry harbours" id="mwrg">Mulberry harbours</a> and an array of specialised tanks nicknamed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hobart's_Funnies" title="Hobart's Funnies" id="mwrw">Hobart's Funnies</a>. In the months leading up to the landings, the Allies conducted <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Bodyguard" title="Operation Bodyguard" id="mwsA">Operation Bodyguard</a>, a substantial <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_deception" title="Military deception" id="mwsQ">military deception</a> that used electronic and visual misinformation to mislead the Germans as to the date and location of the main Allied landings. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolf_Hitler" title="Adolf Hitler" id="mwsg">Adolf Hitler</a> placed  Field Marshal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erwin_Rommel" title="Erwin Rommel" id="mwsw">Erwin Rommel</a> in charge of developing fortifications all along Hitler's proclaimed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall" title="Atlantic Wall" id="mwtA">Atlantic Wall</a> in anticipation of landings in France.</p>

<p id="mwtQ">The Allies failed to accomplish their objectives for the first day, but gained a tenuous foothold that they gradually expanded when they captured the port at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherbourg" title="Cherbourg" id="mwtg">Cherbourg</a> on 26 June and the city of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caen" title="Caen" id="mwtw">Caen</a> on 21 July. A failed counterattack by German forces in response to Allied advances on 7 August left 50,000 soldiers of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7th_Army_(Wehrmacht)" title="7th Army (Wehrmacht)" id="mwuA">German 7th Army</a> trapped in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falaise_pocket" title="Falaise pocket" id="mwuQ">Falaise pocket</a> by 19 August. The Allies launched a second invasion from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mediterranean_Sea" title="Mediterranean Sea" id="mwug">Mediterranean Sea</a> of southern France (code-named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dragoon" title="Operation Dragoon" id="mwuw">Operation Dragoon</a>) on 15 August, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_of_Paris" title="Liberation of Paris" id="mwvA">Liberation of Paris</a> followed on 25 August. German forces retreated east across the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seine" title="Seine" id="mwvQ">Seine</a> on 30 August 1944, marking the close of Operation Overlord.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwvg" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwvw" aria-labelledby="Preparations_for_D-Day"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Preparations_for_D-Day">Preparations for D-Day</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: Preparations for D-Day"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwwA">In June 1940, Germany's leader <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolf_Hitler" title="Adolf Hitler" id="mwwQ">Adolf Hitler</a> had triumphed in what he called "the most famous victory in history"—the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_France" title="Fall of France" class="mw-redirect" id="mwwg">fall of France</a>.<sup about="#mwt127" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005322_34-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005322"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005322-34"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Dear"},"2":{"wt":"Foot"},"3":{"wt":"2005"},"p":{"wt":"322"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005322-34" id="mwww"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxQ">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxg">]</span></span></a></sup> British craft evacuated to England over 338,000 Allied troops trapped along the northern coast of France (including much of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Expeditionary_Force_(World_War_II)" title="British Expeditionary Force (World War II)" id="mwxw">British Expeditionary Force</a>) in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunkirk_evacuation" title="Dunkirk evacuation" id="mwyA">Dunkirk evacuation</a> (27 May to 4 June).<sup about="#mwt130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChurchill1949115_35-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChurchill1949115"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1949115-35"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Churchill"},"2":{"wt":"1949"},"p":{"wt":"115"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1949115-35" id="mwyQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwyg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyw">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwzA">]</span></span></a></sup> British planners reported to Prime Minister <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winston_Churchill" title="Winston Churchill" id="mwzQ">Winston Churchill</a> on 4 October that even with the help of other <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commonwealth_of_Nations" title="Commonwealth of Nations" id="mwzg">Commonwealth</a> countries and the United States, it would not be possible to regain a foothold in continental Europe in the near future.<sup about="#mwt133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200420_36-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200420"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200420-36"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"20"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200420-36" id="mwzw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0Q">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0g">]</span></span></a></sup> After the Axis <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Barbarossa" title="Operation Barbarossa" id="mw0w">invaded the Soviet Union</a> in June 1941, Soviet leader <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Stalin" title="Joseph Stalin" id="mw1A">Joseph Stalin</a> began pressing for a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Front_(World_War_II)#1944–45:_The_Second_Front" title="Western Front (World War II)" id="mw1Q">second front</a> in Western Europe. Churchill declined because he felt that even with American help the British did not have adequate forces to do it,<sup about="#mwt136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga20098–10_37-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga20098–10"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga20098–10-37"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"8–10"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga20098–10-37" id="mw1g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw1w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2A">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2Q">]</span></span></a></sup> and he wished to avoid costly frontal assaults such as those that had occurred at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Somme" title="Battle of the Somme" id="mw2g">Somme</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Passchendaele" title="Battle of Passchendaele" id="mw2w">Passchendaele</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I" title="World War I" id="mw3A">World War I</a>.<sup about="#mwt139" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951582_38-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChurchill1951582"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951582-38"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Churchill"},"2":{"wt":"1951"},"p":{"wt":"582"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951582-38" id="mw3Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw3g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3w">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4A">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mw4Q">Two temporary plans code-named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Roundup_(1942)" title="Operation Roundup (1942)" id="mw4g">Operation Roundup</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Sledgehammer" title="Operation Sledgehammer" id="mw4w">Operation Sledgehammer</a> were put forward for 1942–43, but neither was deemed by the British to be practical or likely to succeed.<sup about="#mwt142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200421–22_39-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200421–22"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200421–22-39"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"21–22"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200421–22-39" id="mw5A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw5Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5g">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5w">]</span></span></a></sup> Instead, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allies_of_World_War_II" title="Allies of World War II" id="mw6A">Allies</a> expanded their activity in the Mediterranean, launching <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Torch" title="Operation Torch" id="mw6Q">Operation Torch</a>, an invasion of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_North_Africa" title="French North Africa" id="mw6g">French North Africa</a>, in November 1942, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allied_invasion_of_Sicily" title="Allied invasion of Sicily" id="mw6w">Allied invasion of Sicily</a> in July 1943, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allied_invasion_of_Italy" title="Allied invasion of Italy" id="mw7A">Allied invasion of Italy</a> in September.<sup about="#mwt145" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200910–11_40-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200910–11"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200910–11-40"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"10–11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200910–11-40" id="mw7Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw7g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7w">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8A">]</span></span></a></sup> These campaigns provided the troops with valuable experience in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphibious_warfare" title="Amphibious warfare" id="mw8Q">amphibious warfare</a>.<sup about="#mwt148" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2012319_41-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2012319"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2012319-41"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2012"},"p":{"wt":"319"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2012319-41" id="mw8g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9A">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw9g">Those attending the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trident_Conference" title="Trident Conference" class="mw-redirect" id="mw9w">Trident Conference</a> in Washington in May 1943 took the decision to launch a cross-Channel invasion within the next year.<sup about="#mwt151" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911_42-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911-42"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911-42" id="mw-A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw-Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-g">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-w">]</span></span></a></sup> Churchill favoured continued Allied operations in the Mediterranean rather than an immediate cross-Channel invasion, as a plan had not yet been devised that was likely to succeed.<sup about="#mwt154" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKimball1984515_43-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKimball1984515"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKimball1984515-43"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kimball"},"2":{"wt":"1984"},"p":{"wt":"515"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKimball1984515-43" id="mw_A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw_Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_g">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_w">]</span></span></a></sup> General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_C._Marshall" title="George C. Marshall" id="mwAQA">George C. Marshall</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Chiefs_of_Staff" title="Joint Chiefs of Staff" id="mwAQE">Joint Chiefs of Staff</a> favoured the cross-channel approach.<sup about="#mwt157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMatloff195921_44-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMatloff195921"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195921-44"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Matloff"},"2":{"wt":"1959"},"p":{"wt":"21"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195921-44" id="mwAQI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQQ">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQU">]</span></span></a></sup> However, historian <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_Matloff" title="Maurice Matloff" id="mwAQY">Maurice Matloff</a> notes that "a number of misconceptions grew up in the postwar period about this Anglo-American debate over strategy," and that "it is a mistake to assume that the British did not from the first want a cross-Channel operation".<sup about="#mwt160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMatloff1969451_45-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMatloff1969451"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff1969451-45"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Matloff"},"2":{"wt":"1969"},"p":{"wt":"451"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff1969451-45" id="mwAQc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQk">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQo">]</span></span></a></sup>  Planners of both nations agreed that an invasion would have to be postponed until German forces were weaker, the required landing craft were available, and logistical infrastructure was in place.<sup about="#mwt163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923_46-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMatloff195923"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923-46"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Matloff"},"2":{"wt":"1959"},"p":{"wt":"23"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923-46" id="mwAQs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ0">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ4">]</span></span></a></sup>  Marshall ultimately conceded the case for continued Mediterranean operations, as long as it did not impede the efforts toward a landing in northern France.<sup about="#mwt166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMatloff195922–23_47-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMatloff195922–23"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195922–23-47"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Matloff"},"2":{"wt":"1959"},"pp":{"wt":"22–23"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195922–23-47" id="mwAQ8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARE">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARI">]</span></span></a></sup> President <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_D._Roosevelt" title="Franklin D. Roosevelt" id="mwARM">Roosevelt</a>'s position was to postpone the decision a few months.<sup about="#mwt169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923_46-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMatloff195923"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923-46"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Matloff"},"2":{"wt":"1959"},"p":{"wt":"23"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMatloff195923-46" id="mwARQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARY">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>  

<p id="mwARg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Brooke,_1st_Viscount_Alanbrooke" title="Alan Brooke, 1st Viscount Alanbrooke" id="mwARk">Alan Brooke, 1st Viscount Alanbrooke</a> appointed British Lieutenant-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_E._Morgan" title="Frederick E. Morgan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARo">Frederick E. Morgan</a> as Chief of Staff, Supreme Allied Commander (COSSAC), to begin detailed planning.<sup about="#mwt172" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911_42-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911-42"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200911-42" id="mwARs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR0">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR4">]</span></span></a></sup> Alanbrooke commented on the operation, saying, "Well there it is, it won't work, but you must bloody well make it".<sup about="#mwt175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHewitt202417_48-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHewitt202417"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHewitt202417-48"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hewitt"},"2":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"17"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHewitt202417-48" id="mwAR8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASE">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASI">]</span></span></a></sup>  The initial plans were constrained by the number of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landing_craft" title="Landing craft" id="mwASM">landing craft</a>, most of which were already committed in the Mediterranean and in the Pacific.<sup about="#mwt178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997177–178,_chart_p._180_49-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997177–178, chart p. 180"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997177–178,_chart_p._180-49"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"177–178, chart p. 180"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997177–178,_chart_p._180-49" id="mwASQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASY">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASc">]</span></span></a></sup> In part because of lessons learned in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dieppe_Raid" title="Dieppe Raid" id="mwASg">Dieppe Raid</a> of 19 August 1942, the Allies decided not to directly assault a heavily defended French seaport in their first landing.<sup about="#mwt181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh20099_50-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh20099"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh20099-50"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"9"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh20099-50" id="mwASk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASs">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASw">]</span></span></a></sup> The failure at Dieppe also highlighted the need for adequate artillery and air support, particularly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Close_air_support" title="Close air support" id="mwAS0">close air support</a>, and specialised ships able to travel extremely close to shore.<sup about="#mwt184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200423_51-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200423"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200423-51"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"23"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200423-51" id="mwAS4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAS8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATA">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATE">]</span></span></a></sup> The short operating range of British aircraft such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supermarine_Spitfire" title="Supermarine Spitfire" id="mwATI">Supermarine Spitfire</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawker_Typhoon" title="Hawker Typhoon" id="mwATM">Hawker Typhoon</a> greatly limited the number of potential landing-sites, as comprehensive air support depended upon having planes overhead for as long as possible.<sup about="#mwt187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989397,_478_52-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGilbert1989397, 478"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989397,_478-52"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gilbert"},"2":{"wt":"1989"},"pp":{"wt":"397, 478"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989397,_478-52" id="mwATQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATY">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATc">]</span></span></a></sup> Morgan considered four sites for the landings: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brittany" title="Brittany" id="mwATg">Brittany</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotentin_Peninsula" title="Cotentin Peninsula" id="mwATk">Cotentin Peninsula</a>, Normandy, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pas-de-Calais" title="Pas-de-Calais" id="mwATo">Pas-de-Calais</a>. As Brittany and Cotentin are peninsulas, the Germans could have cut off the Allied advance at a relatively narrow <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isthmus" title="Isthmus" id="mwATs">isthmus</a>, so these sites were rejected.<sup about="#mwt190" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200913–14_53-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200913–14"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200913–14-53"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"13–14"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200913–14-53" id="mwATw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAT0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT4">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAUA">The Pas-de-Calais, the closest point in continental Europe to Britain, was the location of launch sites for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V-1_flying_bomb" title="V-1 flying bomb" id="mwAUE">V-1</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V-2_rocket" title="V-2 rocket" id="mwAUI">V-2 rockets</a>, then still under development.<sup about="#mwt193" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-55" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-55"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"V-weapons were first launched against the UK on 12 June.{{sfn|Wilmot|1997|p=316}} "}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-55" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwAUM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUU">[</span>e<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUY">]</span></span></a></sup> The Germans regarded it as the most likely initial landing zone and accordingly made it the most heavily fortified region;<sup about="#mwt199" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933–34_56-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200933–34"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933–34-56"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"33–34"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933–34-56" id="mwAUc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUk">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUo">]</span></span></a></sup> however, it offered the Allies few opportunities for expansion as the area is bounded by numerous rivers and canals.<sup about="#mwt202" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170_57-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170-57"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"170"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170-57" id="mwAUs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU0">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU4">]</span></span></a></sup> On the other hand, landings on a broad front in Normandy would permit simultaneous threats against the port of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherbourg" title="Cherbourg" id="mwAU8">Cherbourg</a>, coastal ports further west in Brittany, and an overland attack towards Paris and eventually into Germany. The Allies therefore chose Normandy as the landing site.<sup about="#mwt205" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAmbrose199473–74_58-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEAmbrose199473–74"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEAmbrose199473–74-58"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ambrose"},"2":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"73–74"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAmbrose199473–74-58" id="mwAVA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVI">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVM">]</span></span></a></sup> The most serious drawback of the Normandy coast – the lack of port facilities – would be overcome through the development and deployment of artificial harbours.<sup about="#mwt208" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914_59-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"14"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59" id="mwAVQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVY">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAVg">The COSSAC staff planned to begin the invasion on 1 May 1944.<sup about="#mwt211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170_57-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170-57"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"170"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997170-57" id="mwAVk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVs">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVw">]</span></span></a></sup> The initial draft of the plan was accepted at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Quebec_Conference" title="First Quebec Conference" id="mwAV0">Quebec Conference</a> in August 1943. General Dwight D. Eisenhower was appointed commander of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Headquarters_Allied_Expeditionary_Force" title="Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force" id="mwAV4">Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force</a> (SHAEF).<sup about="#mwt214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989491_60-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGilbert1989491"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989491-60"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gilbert"},"2":{"wt":"1989"},"p":{"wt":"491"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGilbert1989491-60" id="mwAV8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWE">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWI">]</span></span></a></sup> General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Montgomery" title="Bernard Montgomery" id="mwAWM">Bernard Montgomery</a> was named commander of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/21st_Army_Group" title="21st Army Group" id="mwAWQ">21st Army Group</a>, which comprised all of the land forces involved in the invasion.<sup about="#mwt217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13_61-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13-61"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"12–13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13-61" id="mwAWU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWc">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWg">]</span></span></a></sup> On 31 December 1943, Eisenhower and Montgomery first saw the COSSAC plan, which proposed amphibious landings by three <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Division_(military)" title="Division (military)" id="mwAWk">divisions</a>, with two more divisions in support. The two generals immediately insisted on expanding the scale of the initial invasion to five divisions, with airborne descents by three additional divisions, to allow operations on a wider front and to speed up the capture of the port at Cherbourg. This significant expansion required the acquisition of additional landing craft, which caused the invasion to be delayed by a month until June 1944.<sup about="#mwt220" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13_61-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13-61"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"12–13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912–13-61" id="mwAWo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWw">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW0">]</span></span></a></sup> Eventually the Allies committed 39 divisions to the Battle of Normandy: 22 American, 12 British, 3 Canadian, 1 Polish, and 1 French, totalling over a million troops.<sup about="#mwt223" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995684_62-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995684"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995684-62"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Weinberg"},"2":{"wt":"1995"},"p":{"wt":"684"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995684-62" id="mwAW4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAW8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXA">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt226" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004521–533_63-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004521–533"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004521–533-63"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ellis"},"2":{"wt":"Allen"},"3":{"wt":"Warhurst"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"521–533"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004521–533-63" id="mwAXI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXQ">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt229" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-66" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-66"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The British 79th Armoured Division never operated as a single formation,{{sfn|Buckley|2006|p=13}} and thus has been excluded from the total. In addition, a combined total of 16 (three from the 79th Armoured Division) British, Belgian, Canadian, and Dutch independent brigades were committed to the operation, along with four battalions of the Special Air Service.{{sfn|Ellis|Allen|Warhurst|2004|pp=521–523, 524}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-66" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwAXY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXg">[</span>f<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwAXo" aria-labelledby="Allied_invasion_plan"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Allied_invasion_plan">Allied invasion plan</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: Allied invasion plan"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAXs"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAXw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg/500px-Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg/960px-Allied_Invasion_Force.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1265" data-file-height="966" data-file-type="bitmap" height="344" width="450" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.8" id="mwAX0"/></a><figcaption id="mwAX4"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_landings" title="Normandy landings" id="mwAX8">D-day</a> assault routes into Normandy</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAYA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Development_of_Lodgment.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAYE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Development_of_Lodgment.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Development_of_Lodgment.png/500px-Development_of_Lodgment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Development_of_Lodgment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x" data-file-width="800" data-file-height="492" data-file-type="bitmap" height="277" width="450" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.8" id="mwAYI"/></a><figcaption id="mwAYM">Forecast of the development of the lodgement, prepared in February 1944<sup about="#mwt240" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-67" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-67"}}'><a href="#cite_note-67" id="mwAYQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYY">[</span>61<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYc">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAYg">"Overlord" was the name assigned to the establishment of a large-scale <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lodgement" title="Lodgement" id="mwAYk">lodgement</a> on the Continent.<sup about="#mwt241" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951642_68-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChurchill1951642"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951642-68"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Churchill"},"2":{"wt":"1951"},"p":{"wt":"642"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951642-68" id="mwAYo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYw">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAY0">]</span></span></a></sup> The first phase, the amphibious invasion and establishment of a secure foothold, was code-named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_landings" title="Normandy landings" id="mwAY4">Operation Neptune</a><sup about="#mwt244" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914_59-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"14"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59" id="mwAY8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZE">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZI">]</span></span></a></sup> and is often referred to as "D-Day".  To gain the required air superiority needed to ensure a successful invasion, the Allies launched a strategic bombing campaign (codenamed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointblank_directive" title="Pointblank directive" id="mwAZM">Pointblank</a>) to target German aircraft-production, fuel supplies, and airfields. Under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Plan" title="Transport Plan" id="mwAZQ">Transport Plan</a>, communications infrastructure and road and rail links were bombed to cut off the north of France and to make it more difficult to bring up reinforcements. These attacks were widespread so as to avoid revealing the exact location of the invasion.<sup about="#mwt247" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914_59-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"14"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200914-59" id="mwAZU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZc">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZg">]</span></span></a></sup> Elaborate <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_deception" title="Military deception" id="mwAZk">deceptions</a> were planned to prevent the Germans from determining the timing and location of the invasion.<sup about="#mwt250" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093_69-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20093"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69" id="mwAZo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZw">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAZ4">The coastline of Normandy was divided into seventeen sectors, with code-names using a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spelling_alphabet" title="Spelling alphabet" id="mwAZ8">spelling alphabet</a>—from Able, west of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omaha_Beach" title="Omaha Beach" id="mwAaA">Omaha</a>, to Roger on the east flank of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sword_Beach" title="Sword Beach" id="mwAaE">Sword</a>. Eight further sectors were added when the invasion was extended to include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah_Beach" title="Utah Beach" id="mwAaI">Utah</a> on the Cotentin Peninsula. Sectors were further subdivided into beaches identified by the colours Green, Red, and White.<sup about="#mwt253" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuckingham200488_70-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuckingham200488"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuckingham200488-70"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buckingham"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"88"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuckingham200488-70" id="mwAaM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAaQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaU">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAac">Allied planners envisaged preceding the sea-borne landings with airborne drops: near Caen on the eastern flank to secure the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_Orne_(Normandy)" title="River Orne (Normandy)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAag">Orne River</a> bridges, and north of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carentan" title="Carentan" id="mwAak">Carentan</a> on the western flank. The initial goal was to capture Carentan, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isigny-sur-Mer" title="Isigny-sur-Mer" id="mwAao">Isigny</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayeux" title="Bayeux" id="mwAas">Bayeux</a>, and Caen. The Americans, assigned to land at Utah and Omaha, were to cut off the Cotentin Peninsula and capture the port facilities at Cherbourg. The British at Sword and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Beach" title="Gold Beach" id="mwAaw">Gold</a>, and the Canadians at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juno_Beach" title="Juno Beach" id="mwAa0">Juno</a>, were to capture Caen and form a front line from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caumont-l'Éventé" title="Caumont-l'Éventé" id="mwAa4">Caumont-l'Éventé</a> to the south-east of Caen in order to protect the American flank, while establishing airfields near Caen. Possession of Caen and its surroundings would give the Anglo-Canadian forces a suitable staging area for a push south to capture the town of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falaise,_Calvados" title="Falaise, Calvados" id="mwAa8">Falaise</a>. A secure lodgement would be established and an attempt made to hold all territory captured north of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avranches" title="Avranches" id="mwAbA">Avranches</a>-Falaise line during the first three weeks. The Allied armies would then swing left to advance towards the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_Seine" title="River Seine" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAbE">River Seine</a>.<sup about="#mwt256" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951592–593_71-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChurchill1951592–593"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951592–593-71"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Churchill"},"2":{"wt":"1951"},"pp":{"wt":"592–593"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951592–593-71" id="mwAbI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbQ">[</span>65<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt259" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover_72-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map, inside front cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, inside front cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72" id="mwAbY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbg">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt262" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst200478,_81_73-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst200478, 81"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst200478,_81-73"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ellis"},"2":{"wt":"Allen"},"3":{"wt":"Warhurst"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"78, 81"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst200478,_81-73" id="mwAbo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbw">[</span>67<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAb4">The invasion fleet, led by Admiral Sir <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertram_Ramsay" title="Bertram Ramsay" id="mwAb8">Bertram Ramsay</a>, was split into the Western Naval Task Force (under Admiral <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Goodrich_Kirk" title="Alan Goodrich Kirk" id="mwAcA">Alan Kirk</a>) supporting the American sectors and the Eastern Naval Task Force (under Admiral Sir <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Vian" title="Philip Vian" id="mwAcE">Philip Vian</a>) in the British and Canadian sectors.<sup about="#mwt265" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951594_74-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChurchill1951594"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951594-74"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Churchill"},"2":{"wt":"1951"},"p":{"wt":"594"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChurchill1951594-74" id="mwAcI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcQ">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt268" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger19946_75-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger19946"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger19946-75"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"p":{"wt":"6"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger19946-75" id="mwAcY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcg">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAck">]</span></span></a></sup> The American forces of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Army_(United_States)" title="First Army (United States)" id="mwAco">First Army</a>, led by Lieutenant General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Bradley" title="Omar Bradley" id="mwAcs">Omar Bradley</a>, comprised VII Corps (Utah) and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_Corps_(United_States)" title="V Corps (United States)" id="mwAcw">V Corps</a> (Omaha). On the British side, Lieutenant-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miles_Dempsey" title="Miles Dempsey" id="mwAc0">Miles Dempsey</a> commanded the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Army_(United_Kingdom)" title="Second Army (United Kingdom)" id="mwAc4">Second Army</a>, under which <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XXX_Corps_(United_Kingdom)" title="XXX Corps (United Kingdom)" id="mwAc8">XXX Corps</a> was assigned to Gold and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Corps_(United_Kingdom)" title="I Corps (United Kingdom)" id="mwAdA">I Corps</a> to Juno and Sword.<sup about="#mwt271" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009Map,_p._12_76-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009Map, p. 12"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009Map,_p._12-76"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, p. 12"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009Map,_p._12-76" id="mwAdE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdM">[</span>70<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Land forces were under the command of Montgomery, and air command was assigned to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Chief_Marshal" title="Air Chief Marshal" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAdU">Air Chief Marshal</a> Sir <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trafford_Leigh-Mallory" title="Trafford Leigh-Mallory" id="mwAdY">Trafford Leigh-Mallory</a>.<sup about="#mwt274" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200925_77-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200925"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200925-77"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"25"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200925-77" id="mwAdc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdk">[</span>71<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdo">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Canadian_Army" title="First Canadian Army" id="mwAds">First Canadian Army</a> included personnel and units from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polish_Armed_Forces_in_the_West" title="Polish Armed Forces in the West" id="mwAdw">Poland</a>, Belgium, and the Netherlands.<sup about="#mwt277" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilliams1988x_3-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilliams1988x"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams1988x-3"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Williams"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"x"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams1988x-3" id="mwAd0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAd4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd8">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeA">]</span></span></a></sup> Other Allied nations participated.<sup about="#mwt280" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEvans2008623_78-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEvans2008623"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEvans2008623-78"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Evans"},"2":{"wt":"2008"},"p":{"wt":"623"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEvans2008623-78" id="mwAeE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeM">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwAeU" aria-labelledby="Reconnaissance"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Reconnaissance">Reconnaissance</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section: Reconnaissance"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAeY"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAec"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg/500px-Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg/960px-Air_plan_for_landings_in_Normandy_June_1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="A map of southern Britain, northern France and Belgium, marked with the routes the Allied air and naval invasion forces used in the D-Day landings, areas where Allied aircraft patrolled, locations of railway targets that were attacked, and areas where airfields could be built" data-file-width="3451" data-file-height="2225" data-file-type="bitmap" height="226" width="350" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.4" id="mwAeg"/></a><figcaption id="mwAek">Air plan for the Allied landing in Normandy</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAeo">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allied_Expeditionary_Air_Force" title="Allied Expeditionary Air Force" id="mwAes">Allied Expeditionary Air Force</a> flew over 3,200 photo-reconnaissance sorties from April 1944 until 6 June 1944. Photos of the coastline were taken at extremely low altitude to show the invaders the terrain, obstacles on the beach, and defensive structures such as bunkers and gun emplacements. To conceal the location of the invasion, sorties were flown along all European coastline. Inland terrain, bridges, troop emplacements, and buildings were also photographed, in many cases from several angles.<sup about="#mwt283" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200481_79-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200481"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200481-79"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"81"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200481-79" id="mwAew"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAe0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe4">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe8">]</span></span></a></sup> Members of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combined_Operations_Headquarters" title="Combined Operations Headquarters" id="mwAfA">Combined Operations Pilotage Parties</a> clandestinely prepared detailed harbour maps, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depth_sounding" title="Depth sounding" id="mwAfE">depth soundings</a>.<sup about="#mwt286" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200921_80-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200921"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200921-80"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"21"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200921-80" id="mwAfI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfQ">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfU">]</span></span></a></sup> An appeal for holiday pictures and postcards of Europe announced on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC" title="BBC" id="mwAfY">BBC</a> produced over ten million items, some of which proved useful. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_resistance" title="French resistance" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAfc">French resistance</a> provided details on Axis troop movements and on construction techniques used by the Germans for bunkers and other defensive installations.<sup about="#mwt289" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911_81-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911-81"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911-81" id="mwAfg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfo">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAfw">Many German radio messages were encoded using the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma_machine" title="Enigma machine" id="mwAf0">Enigma machine</a> and other enciphering techniques and the codes were changed frequently. A team of code breakers stationed at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bletchley_Park" title="Bletchley Park" id="mwAf4">Bletchley Park</a> worked to break codes as quickly as possible to provide advance information on German plans and troop movements. British military intelligence code-named this information <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultra_(cryptography)" title="Ultra (cryptography)" id="mwAf8">Ultra</a> intelligence as it could only be provided to the most senior commanders. The Enigma code used by Field Marshal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerd_von_Rundstedt" title="Gerd von Rundstedt" id="mwAgA">Gerd von Rundstedt</a>, <i id="mwAgE">Oberbefehlshaber</i> West (Supreme Commander West; <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OB_West" title="OB West" id="mwAgI">OB West</a>), commander of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Front_(World_War_II)" title="Western Front (World War II)" id="mwAgM">Western Front</a>, was broken by the end of March. German intelligence changed the Enigma codes after the Allied landings but by 17 June the Allies were again consistently able to read them.<sup about="#mwt292" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927–28_82-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927–28"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927–28-82"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"27–28"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927–28-82" id="mwAgQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgY">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwAgg" aria-labelledby="Technology"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Technology">Technology</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section: Technology"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAgk"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAgo"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg/500px-Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg/960px-Arromanches_Mulberry-Harbour_Beetle-Pontoons_1_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1575" data-file-height="1049" data-file-type="bitmap" height="226" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwAgs"/></a><figcaption id="mwAgw">Remains of the concrete <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_harbour" title="Mulberry harbour" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAg0">Mulberry harbour</a> B (Gold) at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arromanches-les-Bains" title="Arromanches-les-Bains" id="mwAg4">Arromanches-les-Bains</a> in Normandy, as seen out at sea and on the beach in 1990</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwAg8">After the disastrous <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dieppe_Raid" title="Dieppe Raid" id="mwAhA">Dieppe Raid</a> in August 1942, the Allies developed new technologies for Overlord. To supplement the preliminary offshore bombardment and aerial assaults, some of the landing craft were equipped with artillery and anti-tank guns to provide close supporting fire.<sup about="#mwt295" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997181_83-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997181"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997181-83"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"181"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997181-83" id="mwAhE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhM">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The Allies had decided not to immediately attack any of the heavily protected French ports and two artificial ports, called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_harbour" title="Mulberry harbour" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAhU">Mulberry harbours</a>, were designed by COSSAC planners. Each assembly consisted of a floating outer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakwater_(structure)" title="Breakwater (structure)" id="mwAhY">breakwater</a>, inner concrete <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caisson_(engineering)" title="Caisson (engineering)" id="mwAhc">caissons</a> (called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenix_breakwaters" title="Phoenix breakwaters" id="mwAhg">Phoenix breakwaters</a>) and several floating piers.<sup about="#mwt298" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997183_84-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997183"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997183-84"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"183"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997183-84" id="mwAhk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAho"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhs">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhw">]</span></span></a></sup> The Mulberry harbours were supplemented by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blockship" title="Blockship" id="mwAh0">blockship</a> shelters (codenamed "Gooseberries").<sup about="#mwt301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321_85-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321-85"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"321"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321-85" id="mwAh4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAh8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiA">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiE">]</span></span></a></sup> With the expectation that fuel would be difficult or impossible to obtain on the continent, the Allies built a "Pipe-Line Under The Ocean" (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Pluto" title="Operation Pluto" id="mwAiI">PLUTO</a>). Specially developed pipes <span about="#mwt304" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAiM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"3"},"2":{"wt":"in"}},"i":0}}]}'>3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt304"> </span><span about="#mwt304">in (76</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt304"> </span><span about="#mwt304">mm)</span> in diameter were to be laid under the Channel from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Wight" title="Isle of Wight" id="mwAiQ">Isle of Wight</a> to Cherbourg by D-Day plus 18. Because of technical problems and the delay in capturing Cherbourg, the pipeline was not operational until 22 September. A second line was laid from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeness_(headland)" title="Dungeness (headland)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAiU">Dungeness</a> to Boulogne in late October.<sup about="#mwt305" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200989–90_86-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200989–90"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200989–90-86"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"89–90"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200989–90-86" id="mwAiY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAic"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAig">[</span>80<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAik">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAio">The British built specialised tanks, nicknamed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hobart's_Funnies" title="Hobart's Funnies" id="mwAis">Hobart's Funnies</a>, to deal with conditions expected during the Normandy campaign. Developed under the supervision of Major-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Percy_Hobart" title="Percy Hobart" id="mwAiw">Percy Hobart</a>, these were modified <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M4_Sherman" title="M4 Sherman" id="mwAi0">M4 Sherman</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churchill_tank" title="Churchill tank" id="mwAi4">Churchill tanks</a>. Examples include the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherman_Crab" title="Sherman Crab" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAi8">Sherman Crab</a> tank (equipped with a mine flail), the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churchill_Crocodile" title="Churchill Crocodile" id="mwAjA">Churchill Crocodile</a> (a flame-throwing tank), and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churchill_Ark" title="Churchill Ark" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAjE">Armoured Ramp Carrier</a>, which other tanks could use as a bridge to scale sea-walls or to overcome other obstacles.<sup about="#mwt308" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997182_87-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997182"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997182-87"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"182"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997182-87" id="mwAjI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjQ">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjU">]</span></span></a></sup> In some areas, the beaches consisted of a soft clay that could not support the weight of tanks. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hobart's_Funnies#Designs" title="Hobart's Funnies" id="mwAjY">Bobbin</a> tank unrolled matting over the soft surface, leaving it behind as a route for ordinary tanks.<sup about="#mwt311" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997195_88-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997195"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997195-88"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"195"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997195-88" id="mwAjc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjk">[</span>82<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjo">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armoured_Vehicle_Royal_Engineers" title="Armoured Vehicle Royal Engineers" id="mwAjs">Assault Vehicle Royal Engineers</a> (AVRE) was a Churchill tank modified for many combat engineering tasks, including laying bridges; it was armed with a demolition gun that could fire large charges into <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pillbox_(military)" title="Pillbox (military)" id="mwAjw">pillboxes</a>.<sup about="#mwt314" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009208_89-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009208"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009208-89"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"208"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009208-89" id="mwAj0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAj4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAj8">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Duplex-Drive tank (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DD_tank" title="DD tank" id="mwAkE">DD tank</a>), another design developed by Hobart's group, was a self-propelled amphibious tank kept afloat using a waterproof canvas screen inflated with compressed air.<sup about="#mwt317" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200442–43_90-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200442–43"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200442–43-90"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"42–43"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200442–43-90" id="mwAkI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkQ">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkU">]</span></span></a></sup> These tanks were easily swamped and, on D-Day, many sank before reaching the shore, especially at Omaha.<sup about="#mwt320" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200973_91-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200973"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200973-91"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"73"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200973-91" id="mwAkY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkg">[</span>85<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwAko" aria-labelledby="Deception"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Deception">Deception</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5" title="Edit section: Deception"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt323" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAks" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Military deception"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt324" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt323" id="mwAkw">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_deception" title="Military deception">Military deception</a></div>
<p id="mwAk0">In the months leading up to the invasion, the Allies conducted <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Bodyguard" title="Operation Bodyguard" id="mwAk4">Operation Bodyguard</a>, the overall strategy designed to mislead the Germans as to the date and location of the main Allied landings.<sup about="#mwt325" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995680_92-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995680"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995680-92"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Weinberg"},"2":{"wt":"1995"},"p":{"wt":"680"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWeinberg1995680-92" id="mwAk8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlE">[</span>86<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlI">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Fortitude" title="Operation Fortitude" id="mwAlM">Operation Fortitude</a> included Fortitude North, a misinformation campaign using fake radio-traffic to lead the Germans into expecting an attack on Norway,<sup about="#mwt328" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBrown2007465_93-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBrown2007465"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBrown2007465-93"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Brown"},"2":{"wt":"2007"},"p":{"wt":"465"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBrown2007465-93" id="mwAlQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlY">[</span>87<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlc">]</span></span></a></sup> and Fortitude South, a major deception designed to fool the Germans into believing that the landings would take place at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pas-de-Calais" title="Pas-de-Calais" id="mwAlg">Pas-de-Calais</a> in July. A fictitious <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_U.S._Army_Group" title="First U.S. Army Group" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAlk">First U.S. Army Group</a> was invented, supposedly located in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kent" title="Kent" id="mwAlo">Kent</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sussex" title="Sussex" id="mwAls">Sussex</a> under the command of Lieutenant General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_S._Patton" title="George S. Patton" id="mwAlw">George S. Patton</a>. The Allies constructed dummy tanks, trucks, and landing craft, and positioned them near the coast. Several military units, including II Canadian Corps and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Canadian_Division" title="2nd Canadian Division" id="mwAl0">2nd Canadian Division</a>, moved into the area to bolster the illusion that a large force was gathering there.<sup about="#mwt331" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093_69-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20093"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69" id="mwAl4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAl8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmA">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt334" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200471–72_94-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200471–72"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200471–72-94"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"71–72"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200471–72-94" id="mwAmI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmQ">[</span>88<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmU">]</span></span></a></sup> As well as the broadcast of fake radio-traffic, genuine radio messages from 21st Army Group were first routed to Kent via landline and then broadcast, to give the Germans the impression that most of the Allied troops were stationed there.<sup about="#mwt337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927_95-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927-95"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"27"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927-95" id="mwAmY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmg">[</span>89<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmk">]</span></span></a></sup> Patton remained stationed in England until 6 July, thus continuing to deceive the Germans into believing a second attack would take place at Calais.<sup about="#mwt340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009282_96-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009282"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009282-96"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"282"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009282-96" id="mwAmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmw">[</span>90<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAm0">]</span></span></a></sup> Military and civilian personnel alike were aware of the need for secrecy, and the invasion troops were as much as possible kept isolated, especially in the period immediately before the invasion. American general <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_J._F._Miller" title="Henry J. F. Miller" id="mwAm4">Henry J. F. Miller</a> was sent back to the United States in disgrace after revealing the invasion date at a party.<sup about="#mwt343" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093_69-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20093"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69" id="mwAm8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnE">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAnM">The Germans thought they had an extensive network of spies operating in the UK, but in fact, all their agents had been captured, and some had become <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_agent" title="Double agent" id="mwAnQ">double agents</a> working for the Allies as part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double-Cross_System" title="Double-Cross System" id="mwAnU">Double-Cross System</a>. The double agent <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Pujol_García" title="Juan Pujol García" id="mwAnY">Juan Pujol García</a>, a Spanish opponent of the Nazis known by the code name "Garbo", developed over the two years leading up to D-Day a fake network of informants that the Germans believed were collecting intelligence on their behalf. In the months preceding D-Day, Pujol sent hundreds of messages to his superiors in Madrid, messages specially prepared by the British intelligence service to convince the Germans that the attack would come in July at Calais.<sup about="#mwt346" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927_95-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927-95"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"27"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200927-95" id="mwAnc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAng"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnk">[</span>89<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAno">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt349" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20094_97-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20094"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20094-97"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"4"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20094-97" id="mwAns"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn0">[</span>91<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAn8">Many of the German radar stations on the French coast were destroyed by the RAF in preparation for the landings.<sup about="#mwt352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934_98-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934-98"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"34"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934-98" id="mwAoA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoI">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoM">]</span></span></a></sup> On the night before the invasion, in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Taxable" title="Operation Taxable" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAoQ">Operation Taxable</a>,  <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No._617_Squadron_RAF" title="No. 617 Squadron RAF" id="mwAoU">617 Squadron (the famous "Dambusters")</a> dropped strips of "window", <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaff_(countermeasure)" title="Chaff (countermeasure)" id="mwAoY">metal foil</a> that German radar operators interpreted as a naval convoy approaching Cap d'Antifer (about <span about="#mwt355" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAoc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"80"},"2":{"wt":"km"},"3":{"wt":"mi"}},"i":0}}]}'>80</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt355"> </span><span about="#mwt355">km (50</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt355"> </span><span about="#mwt355">mi)</span> from the actual D-Day landings). The illusion was bolstered by a group of small vessels towing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrage_balloon" title="Barrage balloon" id="mwAog">barrage balloons</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No._218_Squadron_RAF" title="No. 218 Squadron RAF" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAok">No. 218 Squadron RAF</a> also dropped "window" near <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulogne-sur-Mer" title="Boulogne-sur-Mer" id="mwAoo">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Glimmer" title="Operation Glimmer" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAos">Operation Glimmer</a>. On the same night, a small group of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_Air_Service" title="Special Air Service" id="mwAow">Special Air Service</a> (SAS) operators deployed dummy paratroopers over <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Havre" title="Le Havre" id="mwAo0">Le Havre</a> and Isigny. These dummies led the Germans to believe an additional airborne assault had occurred.<sup about="#mwt356" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBickers199419–21_99-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBickers199419–21"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBickers199419–21-99"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bickers"},"2":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"19–21"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBickers199419–21-99" id="mwAo4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAo8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApA">[</span>93<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwApI" aria-labelledby="Rehearsals_and_security"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Rehearsals_and_security">Rehearsals and security</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6" title="Edit section: Rehearsals and security"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwApM"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwApQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg/250px-Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg/500px-Invasion_Training_in_England_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="700" data-file-height="573" data-file-type="bitmap" height="205" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwApU"/></a><figcaption id="mwApY">Training exercise with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_ammunition" title="Live ammunition" class="mw-redirect" id="mwApc">live ammunition</a> </figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwApg">Training exercises for the Overlord landings took place as early as July 1943.<sup about="#mwt359" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200435_100-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200435"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200435-100"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"35"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200435-100" id="mwApk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAps">[</span>94<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApw">]</span></span></a></sup> As the nearby beach resembled the planned Normandy landing-site, the town of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slapton,_Devon" title="Slapton, Devon" id="mwAp0">Slapton in Devon</a> was evacuated in December 1943 and taken over by the armed forces as a site for training exercises, which included the use of landing craft and the management of beach obstacles.<sup about="#mwt362" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199450–51,_54–57_101-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199450–51, 54–57"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199450–51,_54–57-101"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"50–51, 54–57"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199450–51,_54–57-101" id="mwAp4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAp8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqA">[</span>95<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqE">]</span></span></a></sup> A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friendly_fire" title="Friendly fire" id="mwAqI">friendly fire</a> incident there on 27 April 1944 resulted in as many as 450 deaths.<sup about="#mwt365" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELewis1990254_102-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELewis1990254"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELewis1990254-102"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Lewis"},"2":{"wt":"1990"},"p":{"wt":"254"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELewis1990254-102" id="mwAqM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqU">[</span>96<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqY">]</span></span></a></sup> The following day, an additional estimated 749 American soldiers and sailors died when <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E-boat" title="E-boat" id="mwAqc">German torpedo-boats</a> surprised members of Assault Force "U" conducting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exercise_Tiger" title="Exercise Tiger" id="mwAqg">Exercise Tiger</a>.<sup about="#mwt368" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFenton2004_103-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFenton2004"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFenton2004-103"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fenton"},"2":{"wt":"2004"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFenton2004-103" id="mwAqk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqs">[</span>97<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt371" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELewis1990227_104-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELewis1990227"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELewis1990227-104"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Lewis"},"2":{"wt":"1990"},"p":{"wt":"227"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELewis1990227-104" id="mwAq0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAq4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAq8">[</span>98<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArA">]</span></span></a></sup> Exercises with landing craft and live ammunition also took place at the Combined Training Centre in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inveraray" title="Inveraray" id="mwArE">Inveraray</a> in Scotland.<sup about="#mwt374" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200436_105-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZuehlke200436"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200436-105"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zuehlke"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"36"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZuehlke200436-105" id="mwArI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArQ">[</span>99<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArU">]</span></span></a></sup> Naval exercises took place in Northern Ireland, and medical teams in London and elsewhere rehearsed how they would handle the expected waves of casualties.<sup about="#mwt377" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199459,_61_106-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199459, 61"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199459,_61-106"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"59, 61"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199459,_61-106" id="mwArY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArg">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArk">]</span></span></a></sup> Paratroopers conducted exercises, including a huge demonstration drop on 23 March 1944 observed by Churchill, Eisenhower, and other top officials.<sup about="#mwt380" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199461–62_107-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199461–62"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199461–62-107"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"61–62"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199461–62-107" id="mwAro"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArw">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAr4">Allied planners considered tactical surprise to be a necessary element of the plan for the landings.<sup about="#mwt383" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200946_108-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200946"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200946-108"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"46"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200946-108" id="mwAr8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsE">[</span>102<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsI">]</span></span></a></sup> Information on the exact date and location of the landings was provided only to the topmost levels of the armed forces. Men were sealed into their marshalling areas at the end of May, with no further communication with the outside world.<sup about="#mwt386" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930_109-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109" id="mwAsM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsU">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsY">]</span></span></a></sup> Troops were briefed using maps that were correct in every detail except for the place names, and most were not told their actual destination until they were already at sea.<sup about="#mwt389" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930,_36_110-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930, 36"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930,_36-110"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"30, 36"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930,_36-110" id="mwAsc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsk">[</span>104<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAso">]</span></span></a></sup> A news blackout in Britain increased the effectiveness of the deception operations.<sup about="#mwt392" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093_69-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20093"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20093-69" id="mwAss"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs0">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs4">]</span></span></a></sup> Travel to and from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ireland" title="Republic of Ireland" id="mwAs8">Republic of Ireland</a> was banned, and movement within several kilometres of the coast of England restricted.<sup about="#mwt395" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005667_111-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005667"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005667-111"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Dear"},"2":{"wt":"Foot"},"3":{"wt":"2005"},"p":{"wt":"667"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005667-111" id="mwAtA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtI">[</span>105<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwAtQ" aria-labelledby="Weather_forecasting"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Weather_forecasting">Weather forecasting</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Weather forecasting"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt398" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAtU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Weather forecasting for Operation Overlord"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt399" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt398" id="mwAtY">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_forecasting_for_Operation_Overlord" title="Weather forecasting for Operation Overlord">Weather forecasting for Operation Overlord</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAtc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAtg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg/330px-6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg/960px-6thAirdivnormandybriefing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2405" data-file-height="1772" data-file-type="bitmap" height="243" width="330" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.3" id="mwAtk"/></a><figcaption id="mwAto">Men of the British 22nd Independent Parachute Company, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/6th_Airborne_Division_(United_Kingdom)" title="6th Airborne Division (United Kingdom)" id="mwAts">6th Airborne Division</a> being briefed for the invasion, 4–5 June 1944</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAtw">The invasion planners specified a set of conditions regarding the timing of the invasion, deeming only a few days in each month suitable. A full moon was desirable, as it would provide illumination for aircraft pilots and coincide with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_tides" title="Spring tides" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAt0">highest tides</a>. The Allies wanted to schedule the landings shortly before dawn, midway between low and high tide, with the tide coming in. This would improve the visibility of obstacles the enemy had placed on the beach while minimising the time the men would be exposed in the open. Criteria were also specified for wind speed, visibility, and cloud cover.<sup about="#mwt400" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931_112-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112" id="mwAt4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAt8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuA">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuE">]</span></span></a></sup> Eisenhower had tentatively selected 5 June as the date for the assault; however, on 4 June, high winds and heavy seas made it impossible to launch landing craft, and low clouds would prevent aircraft from finding their targets.<sup about="#mwt403" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933_113-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113" id="mwAuI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAuM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuQ">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAuY">By the evening of 4 June, the Allied meteorological team, headed by Group Captain <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Stagg" title="James Stagg" id="mwAuc">James Stagg</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Air_Force" title="Royal Air Force" id="mwAug">Royal Air Force</a>, predicted that the weather would improve sufficiently for the invasion to go ahead on 6 June. Stagg met Eisenhower and other senior commanders at their headquarters at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southwick_House" title="Southwick House" id="mwAuk">Southwick House</a> in Hampshire to discuss the situation.<sup about="#mwt406" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200921_114-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200921"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200921-114"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"21"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200921-114" id="mwAuo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAus"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuw">[</span>108<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAu0">]</span></span></a></sup> General Montgomery and Major-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Bedell_Smith" title="Walter Bedell Smith" id="mwAu4">Walter Bedell Smith</a>, Eisenhower's chief of staff, were eager to launch the invasion. Admiral Bertram Ramsay was prepared to commit his ships, while Air Chief Marshal Trafford Leigh-Mallory expressed concern that the conditions would be unfavourable for Allied aircraft. After much discussion, Eisenhower decided that the invasion should go ahead.<sup about="#mwt409" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997224–226_115-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997224–226"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997224–226-115"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"224–226"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997224–226-115" id="mwAu8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvE">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvI">]</span></span></a></sup> Because the Allies controlled the Atlantic, German meteorologists had less information on incoming weather patterns.<sup about="#mwt412" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934_98-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934-98"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"34"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200934-98" id="mwAvM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvU">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvY">]</span></span></a></sup> As the Luftwaffe meteorological centre in Paris predicted two weeks of stormy weather, many <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wehrmacht" title="Wehrmacht" id="mwAvc">Wehrmacht</a> commanders left their posts to attend war games in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rennes" title="Rennes" id="mwAvg">Rennes</a>, and men in many units were given leave.<sup about="#mwt415" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009131_116-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009131"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009131-116"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"131"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009131-116" id="mwAvk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvs">[</span>110<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvw">]</span></span></a></sup> Marshal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erwin_Rommel" title="Erwin Rommel" id="mwAv0">Erwin Rommel</a> returned to Germany for his wife's birthday and to petition Hitler for more tanks.<sup about="#mwt418" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200942–43_117-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200942–43"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200942–43-117"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"42–43"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200942–43-117" id="mwAv4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAv8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwA">[</span>111<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAwI">Had Eisenhower postponed the invasion again, the next available period with the right combination of tides (but without the desirable full moon) would have been two weeks later, from 18 to 20 June. As it happened, during this period the invaders would have encountered a major storm lasting four days, between 19 and 22 June, which would have prevented the initial landings.<sup about="#mwt421" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933_113-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113" id="mwAwM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwU">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwAwc" aria-labelledby="German_preparations_and_defences"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="German_preparations_and_defences">German preparations and defences</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8" title="Edit section: German preparations and defences"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAwg"><a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05,_Atlantikwall,_Soldaten_der_Legion_"Freies_Indien".jpg' class="mw-file-description" id="mwAwk"><img resource='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05,_Atlantikwall,_Soldaten_der_Legion_"Freies_Indien".jpg' src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05%2C_Atlantikwall%2C_Soldaten_der_Legion_%22Freies_Indien%22.jpg/250px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05%2C_Atlantikwall%2C_Soldaten_der_Legion_%22Freies_Indien%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05%2C_Atlantikwall%2C_Soldaten_der_Legion_%22Freies_Indien%22.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-263-1580-05%2C_Atlantikwall%2C_Soldaten_der_Legion_%22Freies_Indien%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="800" data-file-height="543" data-file-type="bitmap" height="170" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAwo"/></a><figcaption id="mwAws">German troops of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Legion" title="Indian Legion" id="mwAww">Indian Legion</a> on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall" title="Atlantic Wall" id="mwAw0">Atlantic Wall</a> in France, 21 March 1944</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAw4">Nazi Germany had 50 divisions in France and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_Countries" title="Low Countries" id="mwAw8">Low Countries</a> and another 18 stationed in Denmark and Norway.<sup about="#mwt424" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-119" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-119"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" As of November 1943. There were also 206 German divisions on the Eastern Front, 24 in the Balkans, and 22 in Italy.{{sfn|Wilmot|1997|p=144}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-119" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwAxA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxI">[</span>g<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxM">]</span></span></a></sup> Fifteen divisions were in the process of formation in Germany, but there was no strategic reserve.<sup about="#mwt430" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997144_118-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997144"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997144-118"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"144"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997144-118" id="mwAxQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxY">[</span>112<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxc">]</span></span></a></sup> The Calais region was defended by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/15th_Army_(Wehrmacht)" title="15th Army (Wehrmacht)" id="mwAxg">15th Army</a> under <span title="German-language text" about="#mwt433" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAxk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"de"},"2":{"wt":"[[Generaloberst]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="de"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generaloberst" title="Generaloberst">Generaloberst</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_German-language_text" about="#mwt433" id="mwAxo"/> (Colonel General) <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_von_Salmuth" title="Hans von Salmuth" id="mwAxs">Hans von Salmuth</a>, and Normandy by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7th_Army_(Wehrmacht)" title="7th Army (Wehrmacht)" id="mwAxw">7th Army</a> commanded by <i id="mwAx0">Generaloberst</i> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Dollmann" title="Friedrich Dollmann" id="mwAx4">Friedrich Dollmann</a>.<sup about="#mwt434" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200934_120-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200934"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200934-120"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"34"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200934-120" id="mwAx8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyE">[</span>113<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt437" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199413_121-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199413"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199413-121"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199413-121" id="mwAyM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyU">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyY">]</span></span></a></sup> Combat losses throughout the war, particularly on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Front_(World_War_II)" title="Eastern Front (World War II)" id="mwAyc">Eastern Front</a>, had depleted the pool of able young men, so that German soldiers were now on average six years older than their Allied counterparts. Many in the Normandy area were <i id="mwAyg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostlegionen" title="Ostlegionen" id="mwAyk">Ostlegionen</a></i> (eastern legions) conscripts and "volunteers" from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkestan_Legion" title="Turkestan Legion" id="mwAyo">Turkestan</a>,<sup about="#mwt440" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZaloga201358–59_122-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZaloga201358–59"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZaloga201358–59-122"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"pp":{"wt":"58–59"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZaloga201358–59-122" id="mwAys"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAy0">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAy4">]</span></span></a></sup> Russia, Mongolia, and elsewhere. The Wehrmacht had provided them mainly with unreliable captured equipment; they lacked motorised transport.<sup about="#mwt443" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199416–19_123-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199416–19"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199416–19-123"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"pp":{"wt":"16–19"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199416–19-123" id="mwAy8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzE">[</span>116<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzI">]</span></span></a></sup> Formations that arrived later, such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/12th_SS_Panzer_Division_Hitlerjugend" title="12th SS Panzer Division Hitlerjugend" id="mwAzM">12th SS Panzer Division <i id="mwAzQ">Hitlerjugend</i></a>, were, for the most part, younger and far better equipped and trained than the static troops stationed along the coast.<sup about="#mwt446" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200937_124-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200937"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200937-124"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"37"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200937-124" id="mwAzU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzc">[</span>117<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAzk">In early 1944, OB West was significantly weakened by personnel and materiel transfers to the Eastern Front. During the Soviet <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnieper–Carpathian_Offensive" title="Dnieper–Carpathian Offensive" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAzo">Dnieper–Carpathian Offensive</a> (24 December 1943 – 17 April 1944), the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberkommando_des_Heeres" title="Oberkommando des Heeres" id="mwAzs">German High Command</a> was forced to transfer the entire <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/II_SS_Panzer_Corps" title="II SS Panzer Corps" id="mwAzw">II SS Panzer Corps</a> from France, consisting of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th_SS_Panzer_Division_Hohenstaufen" title="9th SS Panzer Division Hohenstaufen" id="mwAz0">9th</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/10th_SS_Panzer_Division_Frundsberg" title="10th SS Panzer Division Frundsberg" id="mwAz4">10th</a> SS Panzer Divisions, as well as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/349th_Infantry_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="349th Infantry Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAz8">349th Infantry Division</a>, 507th Heavy Panzer Battalion and the 311th and 322nd StuG Assault Gun Brigades. All told, the German forces stationed in France were deprived of 45,827 troops and 363 tanks, assault guns, and self-propelled anti-tank guns.<sup about="#mwt449" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015227–228,_235_125-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELiedtke2015227–228, 235"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015227–228,_235-125"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Liedtke"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"pp":{"wt":"227–228, 235"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015227–228,_235-125" id="mwA0A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0I">[</span>118<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0M">]</span></span></a></sup> It was the first major transfer of forces from France to the east since the creation of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Adolf_Hitler's_directives" title="List of Adolf Hitler's directives" id="mwA0Q">Führer Directive 51</a>, which no longer allowed any transfers from the west to the east.<sup about="#mwt452" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015225_126-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELiedtke2015225"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015225-126"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Liedtke"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"p":{"wt":"225"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015225-126" id="mwA0U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0c">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0g">]</span></span></a></sup> There were also transfers to the Italian front: von Rundstedt complained that many of his best units had been sent on a "fool's errand" to Italy, saying it was "madness<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwA0k"> </span>... that frightful boot of a country should have been evacuated<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwA0o"> </span>... we should have held a decent front with a few divisions on the Alpine frontier."<sup about="#mwt455" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilliams2013182_127-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilliams2013182"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams2013182-127"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Williams"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"p":{"wt":"182"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams2013182-127" id="mwA0s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA00">[</span>120<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA04">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA08">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1st_SS_Panzer_Division_Leibstandarte_SS_Adolf_Hitler" title="1st SS Panzer Division Leibstandarte SS Adolf Hitler" id="mwA1A">1st SS Panzer Division Leibstandarte SS Adolf Hitler</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th_Panzer_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="9th Panzer Division (Wehrmacht)" id="mwA1E">9th</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/11th_Panzer_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="11th Panzer Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA1I">11th</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/19th_Panzer_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="19th Panzer Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA1M">19th</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/116th_Panzer_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="116th Panzer Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA1Q">116th</a> Panzer divisions, alongside the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_SS_Panzer_Division_Das_Reich" title="2nd SS Panzer Division Das Reich" id="mwA1U">2nd SS Panzer Division "Das Reich"</a>, had only arrived in March–May 1944 to France for extensive refit after being badly damaged during the Dnieper-Carpathian Offensive. Seven of the eleven panzer or panzergrenadier divisions stationed in France were still not fully operational or only partially mobile in early June 1944.<sup about="#mwt458" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015224–225_128-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELiedtke2015224–225"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015224–225-128"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Liedtke"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"pp":{"wt":"224–225"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELiedtke2015224–225-128" id="mwA1Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1g">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwA1o" aria-labelledby="Atlantic_Wall"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Atlantic_Wall">Atlantic Wall</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9" title="Edit section: Atlantic Wall"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt461" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA1s" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Atlantic Wall"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt462" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt461" id="mwA1w">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall" title="Atlantic Wall">Atlantic Wall</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA10"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantikwall.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA14"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantikwall.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Atlantikwall.png/250px-Atlantikwall.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Atlantikwall.png/500px-Atlantikwall.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="612" data-file-height="804" data-file-type="bitmap" height="328" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA18"/></a><figcaption id="mwA2A"><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt463" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA2E" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"legend-line","href":"./Template:Legend-line"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"#e7c81f solid 3px"},"2":{"wt":"Atlantic Wall"}},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r981673959" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt464" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Legend/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .legend{page-break-inside:avoid;break-inside:avoid-column}.mw-parser-output .legend-color{display:inline-block;min-width:1.25em;height:1.25em;line-height:1.25;margin:1px 0;text-align:center;border:1px solid black;background-color:transparent;color:black}.mw-parser-output .legend-text{}</style></span><div class="legend" about="#mwt463" id="mwA2I"><span class="legend-container" style="background-color: var(--color-inverted-fixed, #fff)"><span class="legend-line mw-no-invert" style="display: inline-block; vertical-align: middle; width: 1.67em; height: 0; border-style: none; border-top: 2px dotted black;border-top:#e7c81f solid 3px;"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Atlantic Wall</div></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwA2M">Alarmed by the raids on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Nazaire_Raid" title="St Nazaire Raid" id="mwA2Q">St Nazaire</a> and Dieppe in 1942, Hitler ordered the construction of fortifications all along the Atlantic coast, from Spain to Norway, to protect against an expected Allied invasion. He envisioned 15,000 emplacements manned by 300,000 troops, but due to shortages, particularly of concrete and manpower, most of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strongpoint" title="Strongpoint" id="mwA2U">strongpoints</a> were never built.<sup about="#mwt465" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930_129-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129" id="mwA2Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2g">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2k">]</span></span></a></sup> As the expected site of an Allied invasion, Pas-de-Calais was heavily defended.<sup about="#mwt468" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930_129-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129" id="mwA2o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2w">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA20">]</span></span></a></sup> In the Normandy area the best fortifications were concentrated at the port facilities at Cherbourg and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Malo" title="Saint-Malo" id="mwA24">Saint-Malo</a>.<sup about="#mwt471" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913_130-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130" id="mwA28"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3E">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3I">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA3M">A report by Rundstedt to Hitler in October 1943 regarding the weak defences in France led to the appointment of Rommel to oversee the construction of further fortifications along the expected invasion-front, which stretched from the Netherlands to Cherbourg.<sup about="#mwt474" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930_129-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200930-129" id="mwA3Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3Y">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3c">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt477" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933_131-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200933"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933-131"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200933-131" id="mwA3g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3o">[</span>124<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3s">]</span></span></a></sup> Rommel was given command of the newly re-formed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Group_B" title="Army Group B" id="mwA3w">Army Group B</a>, which included the 7th Army, the 15th Army, and the forces guarding the Netherlands.<sup about="#mwt480" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199411_132-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199411"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199411-132"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Goldstein"},"2":{"wt":"Dillon"},"3":{"wt":"Wenger"},"4":{"wt":"1994"},"p":{"wt":"11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoldsteinDillonWenger199411-132" id="mwA30"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA34"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA38">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt483" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912_133-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912-133"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"12"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200912-133" id="mwA4E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4M">[</span>126<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4Q">]</span></span></a></sup> Nazi Germany's tangled command structure made it difficult for Rommel to achieve his task. He was not allowed to give orders to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organisation_Todt" title="Organisation Todt" id="mwA4U">Organisation Todt</a>, which was commanded by armaments minister <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Speer" title="Albert Speer" id="mwA4Y">Albert Speer</a>, so in some places he had to assign soldiers to do construction work.<sup about="#mwt486" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913_130-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130" id="mwA4c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4k">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4o">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA4s">Rommel believed that the Normandy coast could be a possible landing point for the invasion, so he ordered the construction of extensive defensive works along that shore. In addition to concrete gun-emplacements at strategic points along the coast, he ordered wooden stakes, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czech_hedgehog" title="Czech hedgehog" id="mwA4w">metal tripods</a>, mines, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cointet-element" title="Cointet-element" id="mwA40">large anti-tank obstacles</a> to be placed on the beach to delay the approach of landing craft and to impede the movement of tanks.<sup about="#mwt489" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56_134-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56-134"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"54–56"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56-134" id="mwA44"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA48"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5A">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5E">]</span></span></a></sup> Expecting the Allies to land at high tide so that the infantry would spend less time exposed on the beach, he ordered many of these obstacles to be placed at the high-tide mark.<sup about="#mwt492" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931_112-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112" id="mwA5I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5Q">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5U">]</span></span></a></sup> Tangles of barbed wire, booby traps, and the removal of ground cover made the approach hazardous for infantry.<sup about="#mwt495" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56_134-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56-134"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"54–56"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200954–56-134" id="mwA5Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5g">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5k">]</span></span></a></sup> On Rommel's order, the number of mines along the coast was tripled.<sup about="#mwt498" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913_130-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130" id="mwA5o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5w">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA50">]</span></span></a></sup> Given the Allied air supremacy (4,029 Allied aircraft assigned to operations in Normandy plus 5,514 aircraft assigned to bombing and defence, versus 570 Luftwaffe planes stationed in France and the Low Countries<sup about="#mwt501" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931_112-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200931-112" id="mwA54"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA58"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6A">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6E">]</span></span></a></sup>), booby-trapped stakes known as <i id="mwA6I">Rommelspargel</i> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rommel's_asparagus" title="Rommel's asparagus" id="mwA6M">Rommel's asparagus</a>) were set up in meadows and fields to deter airborne landings.<sup about="#mwt504" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913_130-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200913-130" id="mwA6Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6Y">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6c">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mwA6g" aria-labelledby="Mobile_reserves"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Mobile_reserves">Mobile reserves</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10" title="Edit section: Mobile reserves"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwA6k">Rommel, believing that the Germans' best chance was to stop the invasion at the shore, requested that mobile reserves—especially tanks—be stationed as close to the coast as possible. Rundstedt, General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_Geyr_von_Schweppenburg" title="Leo Geyr von Schweppenburg" id="mwA6o">Leo Geyr von Schweppenburg</a> (commander of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panzer_Group_West" title="Panzer Group West" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA6s">Panzer Group West</a>), and other senior commanders believed that the invasion could not be stopped on the beaches. Geyr argued for a conventional doctrine: keeping the Panzer formations concentrated in a central position around Paris and Rouen and deploying them only when the main Allied beachhead had been identified.<sup about="#mwt507" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931_135-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931-135"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931-135" id="mwA6w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA60"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA64">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA68">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt510" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915_136-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915-136"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"15"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915-136" id="mwA7A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7I">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7M">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt513" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192_137-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192-137"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"192"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192-137" id="mwA7Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7Y">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7c">]</span></span></a></sup> Geyr also noted that in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_Campaign_(World_War_II)" title="Italian Campaign (World War II)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA7g">Italian Campaign</a> the armour stationed near the coast had been damaged by naval bombardment. Rommel's opinion was that because of the overwhelming Allied air superiority, large-scale movement of tanks would not be possible once the invasion was underway. Hitler made the final decision: he left three divisions under Geyr's command and gave Rommel operational control of three tank-divisions as reserves. Hitler took personal control of four divisions as strategic reserves, not to be used without his direct orders.<sup about="#mwt516" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931_135-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931-135"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200931-135" id="mwA7k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7s">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt519" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915_136-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915-136"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"15"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200915-136" id="mwA70"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA74"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA78">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt522" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192_137-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192-137"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"192"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997192-137" id="mwA8E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8M">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id="11" id="mwA8U" aria-labelledby="Invasion"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Invasion">Invasion</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11" title="Edit section: Invasion"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt525" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA8Y" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Normandy landings"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt526" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt525" id="mwA8c">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_landings" title="Normandy landings">Normandy landings</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA8g"><span id="mwA8k"><video poster="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm/250px--D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser" controls="" preload="none" data-mw-tmh="" height="141" width="250" resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm" data-durationhint="102" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA8o"><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=original" type='video/webm; codecs="vp9, opus"' data-file-width="1920" data-file-height="1080" id="mwA8s"/><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm.240p.vp9.webm" type='video/webm; codecs="vp9, opus"' data-width="426" data-height="240" data-transcodekey="240p.vp9.webm" id="mwA8w"/><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm.360p.mpeg4.mov" type='video/quicktime; codecs="mp4v"' data-width="640" data-height="360" data-transcodekey="360p.mpeg4.mov" id="mwA80"/><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm.480p.vp9.webm" type='video/webm; codecs="vp9, opus"' data-width="854" data-height="480" data-transcodekey="480p.vp9.webm" id="mwA84"/><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b4/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm/D-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm.1080p.vp9.webm" type='video/webm; codecs="vp9, opus"' data-width="1920" data-height="1080" data-transcodekey="1080p.vp9.webm" id="mwA88"/><track kind="subtitles" type="text/vtt" src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/api.php?action=timedtext&amp;title=File%3AD-Day_Invasion_orders_ccLUaOSArrc.webm&amp;lang=en&amp;trackformat=vtt&amp;origin=%2A" srclang="en" label="English ‪(en)‬" data-mwtitle="" data-dir="ltr" id="mwA9A"/></video></span><figcaption id="mwA9E">General Eisenhower's D-Day Invasion orders, presented in a 21st-century video by the U.S. Army</figcaption></figure>

<style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1244412712" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" about="#mwt527" id="mwA9I" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Blockquote/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"blockquote","href":"./Template:Blockquote"},"params":{"text":{"wt":"You are about to embark upon the Great Crusade, toward which we have striven these many months. The eyes of the world are upon you. The hopes and prayers of liberty-loving people everywhere march with you. In company with our brave Allies and brothers-in-arms on other Fronts, you will bring about the destruction of the German war machine, the elimination of Nazi tyranny over the oppressed peoples of Europe, and security for ourselves in a free world."},"sign":{"wt":"Eisenhower"},"source":{"wt":"[[June 6, 1944, order of the day|Letter to Allied Forces]]{{sfn|Whitmarsh|2009|p=42}}"}},"i":0}}]}'>.mw-parser-output .templatequote{overflow:hidden;margin:1em 0;padding:0 32px}.mw-parser-output .templatequotecite{line-height:1.5em;text-align:left;margin-top:0}@media(min-width:500px){.mw-parser-output .templatequotecite{padding-left:1.6em}}</style><blockquote class="templatequote " about="#mwt527"><p>You are about to embark upon the Great Crusade, toward which we have striven these many months. The eyes of the world are upon you. The hopes and prayers of liberty-loving people everywhere march with you. In company with our brave Allies and brothers-in-arms on other Fronts, you will bring about the destruction of the German war machine, the elimination of Nazi tyranny over the oppressed peoples of Europe, and security for ourselves in a free world.</p></blockquote><div class="templatequotecite" about="#mwt527" id="mwA9M"><p style="display: inline; padding-left: 2.3em;">—<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Eisenhower, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/June_6,_1944,_order_of_the_day" title="June 6, 1944, order of the day">Letter to Allied Forces</a><sup about="#mwt529" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200942_138-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200942"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200942-138"}}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200942-138" id="mwA9Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9Y">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9c">]</span></span></a></sup><span style="display:none" data-plural="1"></span></p></div>

<p id="mwA9g">By May 1944, 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwA9k"> </span>million American troops had arrived in the United Kingdom.<sup about="#mwt531" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911_81-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911-81"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200911-81" id="mwA9o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9w">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA90">]</span></span></a></sup> Most were housed in temporary camps in the south-west of England, ready to move across the Channel to the western section of the landing zone. British and Canadian troops were billeted in accommodation further east, spread from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southampton" title="Southampton" id="mwA94">Southampton</a> to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newhaven,_East_Sussex" title="Newhaven, East Sussex" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA98">Newhaven</a>, and even on the east coast for men who would be coming across in later waves. A complex system called Movement Control assured that the men and vehicles left on schedule from twenty departure points.<sup about="#mwt534" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930_109-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109" id="mwA-A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-I">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-M">]</span></span></a></sup> Some men had to board their craft nearly a week before departure.<sup about="#mwt537" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor20091–2_139-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor20091–2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20091–2-139"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"1–2"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor20091–2-139" id="mwA-Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-Y">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-c">]</span></span></a></sup> The ships met at a rendezvous point (nicknamed "Piccadilly Circus") south-east of the Isle of Wight to assemble into convoys to cross the Channel.<sup about="#mwt540" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974_140-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200974"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974-140"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"74"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974-140" id="mwA-g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-o">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-s">]</span></span></a></sup> Minesweepers began clearing lanes on the evening of 5 June,<sup about="#mwt543" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933_113-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200933-113" id="mwA-w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-4">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-8">]</span></span></a></sup> and a thousand bombers left before dawn to attack the coastal defences.<sup about="#mwt546" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200979_141-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200979"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200979-141"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"79"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200979-141" id="mwA_A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_I">[</span>134<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_M">]</span></span></a></sup> Some 1,200 aircraft departed England just before midnight to transport three airborne divisions to their drop zones behind enemy lines several hours before the beach landings.<sup about="#mwt549" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951_142-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200951"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951-142"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"51"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951-142" id="mwA_Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_Y">[</span>135<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_c">]</span></span></a></sup> The American <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82nd_Airborne_Division" title="82nd Airborne Division" id="mwA_g">82nd</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/101st_Airborne_Division" title="101st Airborne Division" id="mwA_k">101st Airborne Divisions</a> were assigned objectives on the Cotentin Peninsula west of Utah. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/6th_Airborne_Division_(United_Kingdom)" title="6th Airborne Division (United Kingdom)" id="mwA_o">British 6th Airborne Division</a> was assigned to capture intact the bridges over the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caen_Canal" title="Caen Canal" id="mwA_s">Caen Canal</a> and River Orne.<sup about="#mwt552" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951–52_143-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200951–52"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951–52-143"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"51–52"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200951–52-143" id="mwA_w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_4">[</span>136<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_8">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_French" title="Free French" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBAA">Free French</a> 4th <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1st_Marine_Infantry_Parachute_Regiment" title="1st Marine Infantry Parachute Regiment" id="mwBAE">SAS</a> battalion of 538 men was assigned objectives in Brittany (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dingson" title="Operation Dingson" id="mwBAI">Operation Dingson</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Samwest" title="Operation Samwest" id="mwBAM">Operation Samwest</a>).<sup about="#mwt555" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECorta1952157–161_144-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECorta1952157–161"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta1952157–161-144"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Corta"},"2":{"wt":"1952"},"pp":{"wt":"157–161"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta1952157–161-144" id="mwBAQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAY">[</span>137<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt558" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECorta199764–79_145-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECorta199764–79"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta199764–79-145"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Corta"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"64–79"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta199764–79-145" id="mwBAg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAo">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAs">]</span></span></a></sup> Some 132,000 men were transported by sea on D-Day, and a further 24,000 came by air.<sup about="#mwt561" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930_109-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109" id="mwBAw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBA0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA4">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA8">]</span></span></a></sup> Preliminary naval bombardment commenced at 05:45 and continued until 06:25 from five battleships, twenty cruisers, sixty-five destroyers, and two monitors.<sup about="#mwt564" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930_109-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200930-109" id="mwBBA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBI">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt567" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200969_146-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200969"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200969-146"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"69"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200969-146" id="mwBBQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBY">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBc">]</span></span></a></sup> Infantry began arriving on the beaches at around 06:30.<sup about="#mwt570" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200970_147-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200970"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200970-147"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"70"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200970-147" id="mwBBg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBo">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="12" id="mwBBw" aria-labelledby="Beaches"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Beaches">Beaches</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12" title="Edit section: Beaches"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt573" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBB0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Utah Beach"},"2":{"wt":"Pointe du Hoc"},"3":{"wt":"Omaha Beach"},"4":{"wt":"Gold Beach"},"5":{"wt":"Juno Beach"},"6":{"wt":"Sword Beach"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt574" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt573" id="mwBB4">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah_Beach" title="Utah Beach">Utah Beach</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointe_du_Hoc" title="Pointe du Hoc">Pointe du Hoc</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omaha_Beach" title="Omaha Beach">Omaha Beach</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Beach" title="Gold Beach">Gold Beach</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juno_Beach" title="Juno Beach">Juno Beach</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sword_Beach" title="Sword Beach">Sword Beach</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBB8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBCA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg/330px-Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg/960px-Troops_move_out_over_the_seawall_on_Utah_Beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2822" data-file-height="2212" data-file-type="bitmap" height="235" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwBCE"/></a><figcaption id="mwBCI">U.S. soldiers of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/8th_Infantry_Regiment_(United_States)" title="8th Infantry Regiment (United States)" id="mwBCM">8th Infantry</a>, 4th Infantry Division advance over the sea-wall at Utah.</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBCQ">The craft bearing the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4th_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="4th Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBCU">U.S. 4th Infantry Division</a> assaulting Utah were pushed by the current to a spot about <span about="#mwt575" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBCY" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1800"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"yd"}},"i":0}}]}'>1,800 metres (2,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt575"> </span><span about="#mwt575">yd)</span> south of their intended landing zone. The troops met light resistance, suffering fewer than 200 casualties.<sup about="#mwt576" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009118_148-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009118"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009118-148"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"118"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009118-148" id="mwBCc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCk">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt579" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHughes20105_149-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHughes20105"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHughes20105-149"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hughes"},"2":{"wt":"2010"},"p":{"wt":"5"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHughes20105-149" id="mwBCs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC0">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC4">]</span></span></a></sup> Their efforts to push inland fell far short of their targets for the first day, but they were able to advance about <span about="#mwt582" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBC8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"4"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"},"order":{"wt":"flip"},"round":{"wt":"0.5"}},"i":0}}]}'>6.5 kilometres (4</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt582"> </span><span about="#mwt582">mi)</span>, making contact with the 101st Airborne Division.<sup about="#mwt583" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover_72-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map, inside front cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, inside front cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72" id="mwBDA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDI">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt586" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200951_150-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200951"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200951-150"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"51"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200951-150" id="mwBDQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDY">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDc">]</span></span></a></sup> The airborne landings west of Utah were not very successful, as only ten per cent of the paratroopers landed in their drop zones. Gathering the men together into fighting units was made difficult by a shortage of radios and by the terrain, with its hedgerows, stone walls and marshes.<sup about="#mwt589" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009166–167_151-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009166–167"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009166–167-151"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"166–167"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009166–167-151" id="mwBDg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDo">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt592" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116_152-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116-152"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"116"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116-152" id="mwBDw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBD0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD4">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD8">]</span></span></a></sup> The 82nd Airborne Division captured its primary objective at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sainte-Mère-Église" title="Sainte-Mère-Église" id="mwBEA">Sainte-Mère-Église</a> and worked to protect the western flank.<sup about="#mwt595" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009115_153-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009115"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009115-153"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"115"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009115-153" id="mwBEE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEM">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Its failure to capture the river crossings at the River <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merderet" title="Merderet" id="mwBEU">Merderet</a> resulted in a delay in sealing off the Cotentin Peninsula.<sup about="#mwt598" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009172_154-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009172"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009172-154"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"172"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009172-154" id="mwBEY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEg">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEk">]</span></span></a></sup> The 101st Airborne Division helped protect the southern flank and captured the lock on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douve" title="Douve" id="mwBEo">River Douve</a> at La Barquette,<sup about="#mwt601" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116_152-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116-152"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"116"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009116-152" id="mwBEs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBE0">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBE4">]</span></span></a></sup> but did not capture the assigned nearby bridges on the first day.<sup about="#mwt604" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009Map,_p._170_155-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009Map, p. 170"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009Map,_p._170-155"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, p. 170"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009Map,_p._170-155" id="mwBE8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFE">[</span>148<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBFM">At <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointe_du_Hoc" title="Pointe du Hoc" id="mwBFQ">Pointe du Hoc</a>, the task for the two hundred men of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Ranger_Battalion_(United_States)" title="2nd Ranger Battalion (United States)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBFU">2nd Ranger Battalion</a>, commanded by Lieutenant Colonel <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Rudder" title="James Rudder" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBFY">James Rudder</a>, was to scale the <span about="#mwt607" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBFc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"30"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"adj":{"wt":"on"},"sigfig":{"wt":"1"}},"i":0}}]}'>30-metre (100</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt607"> </span><span about="#mwt607">ft)</span> cliffs with ropes and ladders to destroy the gun battery located there. While under fire from above, the men scaled the cliff, only to discover that the guns had already been withdrawn. The Rangers located the weapons, unguarded but ready to use, in an orchard some <span about="#mwt608" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBFg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"550"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"yd"}},"i":0}}]}'>550 metres (600</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt608"> </span><span about="#mwt608">yd)</span> south of the point, and disabled them. Under attack, the men at the point became isolated, and some were captured. By dawn on D+1 (7 June), Rudder had only 90 men able to fight. Relief did not come until D+2 (8 June), when members of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/743rd_Tank_Battalion" title="743rd Tank Battalion" id="mwBFk">743rd Tank Battalion</a> arrived.<sup about="#mwt609" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200995–104_156-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200995–104"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200995–104-156"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"95–104"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200995–104-156" id="mwBFo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFw">[</span>149<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBF0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBF4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBF8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg/330px-Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg/960px-Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2963" data-file-height="2385" data-file-type="bitmap" height="241" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwBGA"/></a><figcaption id="mwBGE">The photograph <i id="mwBGI"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Into_the_Jaws_of_Death" title="Into the Jaws of Death" id="mwBGM">Into the Jaws of Death</a></i> shows American troops of Company E of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/16th_Infantry_Regiment_(United_States)" title="16th Infantry Regiment (United States)" id="mwBGQ">16th Infantry Regiment</a>, part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1st_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="1st Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBGU">U.S. 1st Infantry Division</a>, leaving a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Higgins_Boat" title="Higgins Boat" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBGY">Higgins Boat</a> on Omaha.</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBGc">Omaha, the most heavily defended sector, was assigned to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1st_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="1st Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBGg">U.S. 1st Infantry Division</a>, supplemented by troops from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/29th_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="29th Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBGk">U.S. 29th Infantry Division</a>.<sup about="#mwt612" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHughes20105_149-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHughes20105"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHughes20105-149"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hughes"},"2":{"wt":"2010"},"p":{"wt":"5"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHughes20105-149" id="mwBGo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGw">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBG0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt615" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200964–65,_334_157-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200964–65, 334"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200964–65,_334-157"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"64–65, 334"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200964–65,_334-157" id="mwBG4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBG8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHA">[</span>150<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHE">]</span></span></a></sup> They faced the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/352nd_Infantry_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="352nd Infantry Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBHI">352nd Infantry Division</a>, rather than the expected single regiment.<sup about="#mwt618" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200945_158-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200945"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200945-158"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"45"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200945-158" id="mwBHM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHU">[</span>151<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHY">]</span></span></a></sup> Strong currents forced many landing craft east of their intended position or delayed them. Casualties were heavier than all the other landings combined, as the men were subjected to fire from the cliffs above.<sup about="#mwt621" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200976–77,_334_159-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200976–77, 334"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200976–77,_334-159"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"76–77, 334"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200976–77,_334-159" id="mwBHc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHk">[</span>152<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHo">]</span></span></a></sup> Problems clearing the beach of obstructions led to the beachmaster calling a halt to further landings of vehicles at 08:30. A group of destroyers arrived around this time to offer supporting artillery fire.<sup about="#mwt624" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200990–91_160-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200990–91"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200990–91-160"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"90–91"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200990–91-160" id="mwBHs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBH0">[</span>153<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBH4">]</span></span></a></sup> Exit from Omaha was possible only via five gullies, and by late morning barely six hundred men had reached the higher ground. By noon, as the artillery fire took its toll and the Germans started to run out of ammunition, the Americans were able to clear some lanes on the beaches. They also started clearing the draws of enemy defences so that vehicles could move off the beach.<sup about="#mwt627" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200956,_83_161-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200956, 83"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200956,_83-161"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"56, 83"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200956,_83-161" id="mwBH8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIE">[</span>154<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBII">]</span></span></a></sup> The tenuous beachhead was expanded over the following days, and the D-Day objectives were accomplished by D+3 (9 June).<sup about="#mwt630" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009337_162-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009337"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009337-162"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"337"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009337-162" id="mwBIM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIU">[</span>155<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBIc">At Gold, high winds made conditions difficult for the landing craft, and the amphibious DD tanks were landed close to shore or directly on the beach instead of further out as planned.<sup about="#mwt633" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009281–282_163-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009281–282"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009281–282-163"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"281–282"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009281–282-163" id="mwBIg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIo">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIs">]</span></span></a></sup> Aerial attacks had failed to hit the Le Hamel strongpoint, and its <span about="#mwt636" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBIw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"75"},"2":{"wt":"mm"},"sigfig":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'>75</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt636"> </span><span about="#mwt636">mm (2.95</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt636"> </span><span about="#mwt636">in)</span> gun continued to do damage until 16:00. On the western flank, the 1st Battalion, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Hampshire_Regiment" title="Royal Hampshire Regiment" id="mwBI0">Hampshire Regiment</a> captured <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arromanches" title="Arromanches" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBI4">Arromanches</a> (future site of Mulberry "B"), and contact was made on the eastern flank with the Canadian forces at Juno.<sup about="#mwt637" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997270–273_164-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997270–273"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997270–273-164"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"270–273"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997270–273-164" id="mwBI8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJE">[</span>157<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBJM"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBJQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg/330px-The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg/960px-The_Invasion_of_Normandy_1944_B5258_retouched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1250" data-file-height="1108" data-file-type="bitmap" height="266" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwBJU"/></a><figcaption id="mwBJY">Gold Beach on 7 June 1944</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBJc">Landings of infantry at Juno were delayed because of rough seas, and the men arrived ahead of their supporting armour, suffering many casualties while disembarking. Most of the offshore bombardment had missed the German defences. In spite of these difficulties, the Canadians quickly cleared the beach and created two exits to the villages above. Delays in taking <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bény-sur-Mer" title="Bény-sur-Mer" id="mwBJg">Bény-sur-Mer</a> led to congestion on the beach, but by nightfall, the contiguous Juno and Gold beachheads covered an area <span about="#mwt640" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBJk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"12"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"},"order":{"wt":"flip"}},"i":0}}]}'>19 kilometres (12</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt640"> </span><span about="#mwt640">mi)</span> wide and <span about="#mwt641" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBJo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"7"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"},"order":{"wt":"flip"},"sigfig":{"wt":"1"}},"i":0}}]}'>10 kilometres (7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt641"> </span><span about="#mwt641">mi)</span> deep.<sup about="#mwt642" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997275–276_165-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997275–276"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997275–276-165"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"275–276"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997275–276-165" id="mwBJs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJ0">[</span>158<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJ4">]</span></span></a></sup> One troop of the 1st Hussar Tank Regiment was the only Allied unit to achieve its objective on the first day of the invasion.<sup about="#mwt645" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDrez2005_166-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEDrez2005"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEDrez2005-166"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Drez"},"2":{"wt":"2005"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDrez2005-166" id="mwBJ8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKE">[</span>159<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKI">]</span></span></a></sup> Casualties at Juno were 961 men.<sup about="#mwt648" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009131_167-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009131"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009131-167"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"131"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009131-167" id="mwBKM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKU">[</span>160<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBKc">On Sword, 21 of 25 DD tanks succeeded in getting safely ashore to provide cover for the infantry, who began disembarking at 07:30. They quickly cleared the beach and created several exits for the tanks. In the windy conditions, the tide came in more quickly than expected, making manoeuvring the armour difficult.<sup about="#mwt651" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997277–278_168-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997277–278"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997277–278-168"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"277–278"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997277–278-168" id="mwBKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKo">[</span>161<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKs">]</span></span></a></sup> The 2nd Battalion, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King's_Shropshire_Light_Infantry" title="King's Shropshire Light Infantry" id="mwBKw">King's Shropshire Light Infantry</a> advanced on foot to within a few kilometres of Caen, but had to withdraw due to lack of armour support.<sup about="#mwt654" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009143,_148_169-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009143, 148"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009143,_148-169"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"143, 148"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009143,_148-169" id="mwBK0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBK4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK8">[</span>162<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLA">]</span></span></a></sup> At 16:00, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/21st_Panzer_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="21st Panzer Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBLE">German 21st Panzer Division</a> mounted a counterattack between Sword and Juno and nearly succeeded in reaching the coast. They met stiff resistance from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3rd_Division_(United_Kingdom)" title="3rd Division (United Kingdom)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBLI">British 3rd Infantry Division</a> and were soon recalled to assist in the area between Caen and Bayeux.<sup about="#mwt657" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009326–327_170-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009326–327"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009326–327-170"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"326–327"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009326–327-170" id="mwBLM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLU">[</span>163<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt660" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997283_171-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997283"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997283-171"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"283"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997283-171" id="mwBLc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLk">[</span>164<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBLs"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_Infantry_Division,_E-1_draw,_Easy_Red_sector,_Omaha_Beach,_D+1,_June_7,_1944.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBLw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_Infantry_Division,_E-1_draw,_Easy_Red_sector,_Omaha_Beach,_D+1,_June_7,_1944.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/2nd_Infantry_Division%2C_E-1_draw%2C_Easy_Red_sector%2C_Omaha_Beach%2C_D%2B1%2C_June_7%2C_1944.jpg/500px-2nd_Infantry_Division%2C_E-1_draw%2C_Easy_Red_sector%2C_Omaha_Beach%2C_D%2B1%2C_June_7%2C_1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/2nd_Infantry_Division%2C_E-1_draw%2C_Easy_Red_sector%2C_Omaha_Beach%2C_D%2B1%2C_June_7%2C_1944.jpg/960px-2nd_Infantry_Division%2C_E-1_draw%2C_Easy_Red_sector%2C_Omaha_Beach%2C_D%2B1%2C_June_7%2C_1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2640" data-file-height="1824" data-file-type="bitmap" height="263" width="380" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.5" id="mwBL0"/></a><figcaption id="mwBL4">The build-up at Omaha Beach: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="2nd Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBL8">U.S. 2nd Infantry Division</a> troops and equipment moving inland toward <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Laurent-sur-Mer" title="Saint-Laurent-sur-Mer" id="mwBMA">Saint-Laurent-sur-Mer</a> on D+1, 7 June 1944.</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBME">The first components of the artificial <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_harbour" title="Mulberry harbour" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBMI">Mulberry harbours</a> were brought across on D+1 (7 June) and the structures were in use for unloading by mid-June.<sup about="#mwt663" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321_85-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321-85"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"321"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997321-85" id="mwBMM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMU">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMY">]</span></span></a></sup> One was constructed at Arromanches by the British, the other at Omaha by the Americans. Severe storms on 19 June interrupted the landing of supplies and destroyed the Omaha harbour.<sup about="#mwt666" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009215–216_172-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009215–216"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009215–216-172"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"215–216"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009215–216-172" id="mwBMc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMk">[</span>165<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMo">]</span></span></a></sup> The repaired Arromanches harbour was able to receive around 6,000 tons of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Materiel" title="Materiel" id="mwBMs">materiel</a> daily and was in continuous use for the next ten months, but most shipments were brought in over the beaches until the port of Cherbourg was cleared of mines and obstructions on 16 July.<sup about="#mwt669" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997387_173-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997387"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997387-173"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"387"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997387-173" id="mwBMw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBM0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM4">[</span>166<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt672" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009331_174-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009331"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009331-174"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"331"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009331-174" id="mwBNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNI">[</span>167<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBNQ">Allied casualties on the first day were at least 10,000, with 4,414 confirmed dead.<sup about="#mwt675" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200987_175-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200987"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200987-175"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"87"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200987-175" id="mwBNU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNc">[</span>168<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNg">]</span></span></a></sup> The Germans lost 1,000 men.<sup about="#mwt678" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009335_176-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009335"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009335-176"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"335"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009335-176" id="mwBNk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNs">[</span>169<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNw">]</span></span></a></sup> The Allied invasion plans had called for the capture of Carentan, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Lô" title="St. Lô" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBN0">St. Lô</a>, Caen, and Bayeux on the first day, with all the beaches (other than Utah), linked with a front line <span about="#mwt681" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBN4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"10"},"2":{"wt":"to"},"3":{"wt":"16"},"4":{"wt":"km"},"5":{"wt":"mi"},"sigfig":{"wt":"1"}},"i":0}}]}'>10 to 16 kilometres (6 to 10</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt681"> </span><span about="#mwt681">mi)</span> from the beaches; none of these objectives were achieved.<sup about="#mwt682" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover_72-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map, inside front cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, inside front cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_inside_front_cover-72" id="mwBN8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOE">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOI">]</span></span></a></sup> The five bridgeheads were not connected until 12 June, by which time the Allies held a front around <span about="#mwt685" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBOM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"97"},"2":{"wt":"km"}},"i":0}}]}'>97 kilometres (60</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt685"> </span><span about="#mwt685">mi)</span> long and <span about="#mwt686" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBOQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"24"},"2":{"wt":"km"}},"i":0}}]}'>24 kilometres (15</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt686"> </span><span about="#mwt686">mi)</span> deep.<sup about="#mwt687" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHorn201013_177-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHorn201013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHorn201013-177"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Horn"},"2":{"wt":"2010"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHorn201013-177" id="mwBOU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOc">[</span>170<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOg">]</span></span></a></sup> Caen, a major objective, was still in German hands at the end of D-Day and would not be completely captured until 21 July.<sup about="#mwt690" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997360_178-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997360"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997360-178"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"360"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997360-178" id="mwBOk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOs">[</span>171<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOw">]</span></span></a></sup> Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the English Channel on 6 June, and more than two<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBO0"> </span>million Allied troops were in France by the end of August.<sup about="#mwt693" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005627–630_179-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005627–630"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005627–630-179"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Dear"},"2":{"wt":"Foot"},"3":{"wt":"2005"},"pp":{"wt":"627–630"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDearFoot2005627–630-179" id="mwBO4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBO8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPA">[</span>172<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="13" id="mwBPI" aria-labelledby="Cherbourg"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Cherbourg">Cherbourg</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13" title="Edit section: Cherbourg"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt696" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBPM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Battle of Cherbourg"},"2":{"wt":"Bombardment of Cherbourg"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt697" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt696" id="mwBPQ">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cherbourg" title="Battle of Cherbourg">Battle of Cherbourg</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombardment_of_Cherbourg" title="Bombardment of Cherbourg">Bombardment of Cherbourg</a></div>

<p id="mwBPU">In the western part of the lodgement, US troops were to occupy the Cotentin Peninsula, especially Cherbourg, which would provide the Allies with a deep water harbour. The terrain behind Utah and Omaha was characterised by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocage" title="Bocage" id="mwBPY">bocage</a>, with thorny hedgerows on embankments <span about="#mwt698" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBPc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"3"},"2":{"wt":"to"},"3":{"wt":"4"},"4":{"wt":"ft"},"5":{"wt":"m"},"order":{"wt":"flip"},"6":{"wt":"1"}},"i":0}}]}'>0.9 to 1.2 metres (3 to 4</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt698"> </span><span about="#mwt698">ft)</span> high with a ditch on either side.<sup about="#mwt699" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301_180-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301-180"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"301"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301-180" id="mwBPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPo">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPs">]</span></span></a></sup> Many areas were additionally protected by rifle pits and machine-gun emplacements.<sup about="#mwt702" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009175_181-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009175"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009175-181"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"175"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009175-181" id="mwBPw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBP0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP4">[</span>174<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP8">]</span></span></a></sup> Most of the roads were too narrow for tanks.<sup about="#mwt705" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301_180-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301-180"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"301"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997301-180" id="mwBQA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQI">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQM">]</span></span></a></sup> The Germans had flooded the fields behind Utah with sea water for up to <span about="#mwt708" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBQQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"},"order":{"wt":"flip"},"4":{"wt":"0"}},"i":0}}]}'>3 kilometres (2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt708"> </span><span about="#mwt708">mi)</span> from the coast.<sup about="#mwt709" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200949_182-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200949"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200949-182"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"49"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh200949-182" id="mwBQU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQc">[</span>175<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQg">]</span></span></a></sup> German forces on the peninsula included the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/91st_Infantry_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="91st Infantry Division (Wehrmacht)" id="mwBQk">91st Infantry Division</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/243rd_Static_Infantry_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="243rd Static Infantry Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBQo">243rd</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/709th_Static_Infantry_Division_(Wehrmacht)" title="709th Static Infantry Division (Wehrmacht)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBQs">709th Static Infantry Divisions</a>.<sup about="#mwt712" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009118–120_183-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009118–120"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009118–120-183"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"118–120"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009118–120-183" id="mwBQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQ4">[</span>176<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQ8">]</span></span></a></sup> By D+3 the Allied commanders realised that Cherbourg would not quickly be taken, and decided to cut off the peninsula to prevent any further reinforcements from being brought in.<sup about="#mwt715" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009179_184-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009179"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009179-184"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"179"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009179-184" id="mwBRA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRI">[</span>177<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRM">]</span></span></a></sup> After failed attempts by the inexperienced <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/90th_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="90th Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBRQ">90th Infantry Division</a>, Major General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._Lawton_Collins" title="J. Lawton Collins" id="mwBRU">J. Lawton Collins</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VII_Corps_(United_States)" title="VII Corps (United States)" id="mwBRY">VII Corps</a> commander, assigned the veteran <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="9th Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBRc">9th Infantry Division</a> to the task. They reached the west coast of the Cotentin on 17 June, cutting off Cherbourg.<sup about="#mwt718" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009182_185-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009182"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009182-185"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"182"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009182-185" id="mwBRg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRo">[</span>178<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRs">]</span></span></a></sup> The 9th Division, joined by the 4th and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/79th_Infantry_Division_(United_States)" title="79th Infantry Division (United States)" id="mwBRw">79th Infantry Divisions</a>, took control of the peninsula in fierce fighting from 19 June; Cherbourg was captured on 26 June. By this time, the Germans had destroyed the port facilities, which were not brought back into full operation until September.<sup about="#mwt721" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009185–193_186-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009185–193"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009185–193-186"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"185–193"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009185–193-186" id="mwBR0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBR4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBR8">[</span>179<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="14" id="mwBSE" aria-labelledby="Caen"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Caen">Caen</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14" title="Edit section: Caen"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt724" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBSI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Battle for Caen"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt725" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt724" id="mwBSM">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_for_Caen" title="Battle for Caen">Battle for Caen</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBSQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Battleforceanmapenglish.PNG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBSU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Battleforceanmapenglish.PNG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Battleforceanmapenglish.PNG/500px-Battleforceanmapenglish.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Battleforceanmapenglish.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x" data-file-width="700" data-file-height="461" data-file-type="bitmap" height="231" width="350" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.4" id="mwBSY"/></a><figcaption id="mwBSc">Operations in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_for_Caen" title="Battle for Caen" id="mwBSg">Battle for Caen</a></figcaption></figure><p id="mwBSk">Fighting in the Caen area versus the 21st Panzer, the 12th SS Panzer Division <i id="mwBSo">Hitlerjugend</i> and other units soon reached a stalemate.<sup about="#mwt726" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009186_187-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009186"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009186-187"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"186"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009186-187" id="mwBSs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBS0">[</span>180<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBS4">]</span></span></a></sup> During <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Perch" title="Operation Perch" id="mwBS8">Operation Perch</a>, XXX Corps attempted to advance south towards <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mont_Pinçon" title="Mont Pinçon" id="mwBTA">Mont Pinçon</a> but soon abandoned the direct approach in favour of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pincer_attack" title="Pincer attack" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBTE">pincer attack</a> to encircle Caen. XXX Corps made a flanking move from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tilly-sur-Seulles" title="Tilly-sur-Seulles" id="mwBTI">Tilly-sur-Seulles</a> towards <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villers-Bocage,_Calvados" title="Villers-Bocage, Calvados" id="mwBTM">Villers-Bocage</a> with part of the 7th Armoured Division, while I Corps tried to pass Caen to the east. The attack by I Corps was quickly halted and XXX Corps briefly captured Villers-Bocage. Advanced elements of the British force were ambushed, initiating a day-long <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Villers-Bocage" title="Battle of Villers-Bocage" id="mwBTQ">Battle of Villers-Bocage</a> and then the Battle of the Box. The British were forced to withdraw to Tilly-sur-Seulles.<sup about="#mwt729" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004247–254_188-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004247–254"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004247–254-188"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ellis"},"2":{"wt":"Allen"},"3":{"wt":"Warhurst"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"247–254"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004247–254-188" id="mwBTU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTc">[</span>181<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt732" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEForty200436,_97_189-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEForty200436, 97"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEForty200436,_97-189"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Forty"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"36, 97"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEForty200436,_97-189" id="mwBTk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTs">[</span>182<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTw">]</span></span></a></sup> After a delay because of storms from 17 to 23 June, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Epsom" title="Operation Epsom" id="mwBT0">Operation Epsom</a> began on 26 June, an attempt by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VIII_Corps_(United_Kingdom)" title="VIII Corps (United Kingdom)" id="mwBT4">VIII Corps</a> to swing around and attack Caen from the south-west and establish a bridgehead south of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odon_(river)" title="Odon (river)" id="mwBT8">Odon</a>.<sup about="#mwt735" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997342_190-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997342-190"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997342-190" id="mwBUA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUI">[</span>183<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUM">]</span></span></a></sup> Although the operation failed its objectives, the Germans suffered many tank losses after committing every available Panzer unit to the operation.<sup about="#mwt738" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009232–237_191-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009232–237"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009232–237-191"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"232–237"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009232–237-191" id="mwBUQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUY">[</span>184<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUc">]</span></span></a></sup> Rundstedt was dismissed on 1 July and replaced as OB West by Field Marshal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Günther_von_Kluge" title="Günther von Kluge" id="mwBUg">Günther von Kluge</a> after remarking that the war was now lost.<sup about="#mwt741" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997347_192-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997347"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997347-192"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"347"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997347-192" id="mwBUk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUs">[</span>185<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUw">]</span></span></a></sup> The northern suburbs of Caen were bombed on the evening of 7 July and then occupied north of the River Orne in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Charnwood" title="Operation Charnwood" id="mwBU0">Operation Charnwood</a> on 8–9 July.<sup about="#mwt744" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECopp200073_193-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECopp200073"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp200073-193"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Copp"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"p":{"wt":"73"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp200073-193" id="mwBU4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBU8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVA">[</span>186<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt747" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009273_194-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009273"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009273-194"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"273"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009273-194" id="mwBVI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVQ">[</span>187<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVU">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Atlantic" title="Operation Atlantic" id="mwBVY">Operation Atlantic</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Goodwood" title="Operation Goodwood" id="mwBVc">Operation Goodwood</a> captured the rest of Caen and the high ground to the south from 18 to 21 July, by which time the city was nearly destroyed.<sup about="#mwt750" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009340–341_195-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009340–341"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009340–341-195"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"340–341"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009340–341-195" id="mwBVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVo">[</span>188<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVs">]</span></span></a></sup> Hitler survived an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/20_July_plot" title="20 July plot" id="mwBVw">assassination attempt</a> on 20 July.<sup about="#mwt753" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009332–333_196-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009332–333"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009332–333-196"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"332–333"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009332–333-196" id="mwBV0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBV4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBV8">[</span>189<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwBWE" aria-labelledby="Breakout_from_the_beachhead"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Breakout_from_the_beachhead">Breakout from the beachhead</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15" title="Edit section: Breakout from the beachhead"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBWI"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Normandy_Breakout_Map,_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBWM"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Normandy_Breakout_Map,_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Normandy_Breakout_Map%2C_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg/500px-Normandy_Breakout_Map%2C_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Normandy_Breakout_Map%2C_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg/960px-Normandy_Breakout_Map%2C_operations_25_July_to_15_August_1944.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="395" data-file-type="drawing" height="270" width="350" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.4" id="mwBWQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwBWU">Map showing the breakout from the Normandy beachhead and the formation of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falaise_Pocket" title="Falaise Pocket" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBWY">Falaise Pocket</a>, August 1944</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBWc">After securing territory in the Cotentin Peninsula south as far as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Lô" title="Saint-Lô" id="mwBWg">Saint-Lô</a>, the U.S. First Army launched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Cobra" title="Operation Cobra" id="mwBWk">Operation Cobra</a> on 25 July and advanced further south to Avranches by 1 August.<sup about="#mwt756" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_p._344_197-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map, p. 344"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_p._344-197"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"loc":{"wt":"Map, p. 344"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009Map,_p._344-197" id="mwBWo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBWs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWw">[</span>190<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBW0">]</span></span></a></sup> The British launched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Bluecoat" title="Operation Bluecoat" id="mwBW4">Operation Bluecoat</a> on 30 July to secure <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vire" title="Vire" id="mwBW8">Vire</a> and the high ground of Mont Pinçon.<sup about="#mwt759" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009366–367_198-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009366–367"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009366–367-198"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"366–367"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009366–367-198" id="mwBXA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXI">[</span>191<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXM">]</span></span></a></sup> Lieutenant General Patton's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Army_Central" title="United States Army Central" id="mwBXQ">U.S. Third Army</a>, activated on 1 August, quickly took most of Brittany and territory as far south as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loire" title="Loire" id="mwBXU">Loire</a>, while the First Army maintained pressure eastward toward <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Mans" title="Le Mans" id="mwBXY">Le Mans</a> to protect their flank. By 3 August, Patton and the Third Army were able to leave a small force in Brittany and drive eastward towards the main concentration of German forces south of Caen.<sup about="#mwt762" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997398–400_199-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997398–400"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997398–400-199"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"398–400"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997398–400-199" id="mwBXc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXk">[</span>192<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXo">]</span></span></a></sup> Over Kluge's objections, on 4 August Hitler ordered a counter-offensive (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Lüttich" title="Operation Lüttich" id="mwBXs">Operation Lüttich</a>) from Vire towards Avranches.<sup about="#mwt765" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997399–400_200-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997399–400"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997399–400-200"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"399–400"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997399–400-200" id="mwBXw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBX0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBX4">[</span>193<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBX8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBYA">While <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/II_Canadian_Corps" title="II Canadian Corps" id="mwBYE">II Canadian Corps</a> pushed south from Caen toward Falaise in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Totalize" title="Operation Totalize" id="mwBYI">Operation Totalise</a> on 8 August,<sup about="#mwt768" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997410_201-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997410"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997410-201"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"410"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997410-201" id="mwBYM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBYQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYU">[</span>194<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYY">]</span></span></a></sup> Bradley and Montgomery realised that there was an opportunity for the bulk of the German forces to be <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falaise_pocket" title="Falaise pocket" id="mwBYc">trapped at Falaise</a>. The Third Army continued the encirclement from the south, reaching <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alençon" title="Alençon" id="mwBYg">Alençon</a> on 11 August. Although Hitler continued to insist until 14 August that his forces should counter-attack, Kluge and his officers began planning a retreat eastward.<sup about="#mwt771" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009434–435_202-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009434–435"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009434–435-202"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"434–435"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009434–435-202" id="mwBYk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBYo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYs">[</span>195<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYw">]</span></span></a></sup> The German forces were severely hampered by Hitler's insistence on making all major decisions himself, which left his forces without orders for periods as long as 24 hours while information was sent back and forth to the Führer's residence at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obersalzberg" title="Obersalzberg" id="mwBY0">Obersalzberg</a> in Bavaria.<sup about="#mwt774" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997416–417_203-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997416–417"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997416–417-203"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"416–417"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997416–417-203" id="mwBY4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBY8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZA">[</span>196<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZE">]</span></span></a></sup> On the evening of 12 August, Patton asked Bradley if his forces should continue northward to close the gap and encircle the German forces. Bradley refused because Montgomery had already assigned the First Canadian Army to take the territory from the north.<sup about="#mwt777" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009440_204-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009440"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009440-204"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"440"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009440-204" id="mwBZI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZQ">[</span>197<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt780" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997418_205-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997418"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997418-205"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"418"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997418-205" id="mwBZY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZg">[</span>198<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZk">]</span></span></a></sup> The Canadians met heavy resistance and captured Falaise on 16 August. The gap was closed on 21 August, trapping 50,000 German troops but more than a third of the German 7th Army and the remnants of nine of the eleven Panzer divisions had escaped to the east.<sup about="#mwt783" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997420_206-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997420"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997420-206"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"420"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997420-206" id="mwBZo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZw">[</span>199<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZ0">]</span></span></a></sup> Montgomery's decision-making regarding the Falaise Gap was criticised at the time by American commanders, especially Patton, although Bradley was more sympathetic and believed Patton would not have been able to close the gap.<sup about="#mwt786" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBradley1951377_207-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBradley1951377"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBradley1951377-207"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bradley"},"2":{"wt":"1951"},"p":{"wt":"377"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBradley1951377-207" id="mwBZ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaA">[</span>200<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaE">]</span></span></a></sup> The issue has been the subject of much discussion among historians, criticism being levelled at American, British and Canadian forces.<sup about="#mwt789" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009439–440_208-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009439–440"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009439–440-208"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"439–440"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009439–440-208" id="mwBaI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBaM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaQ">[</span>201<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt792" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997424_209-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997424"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997424-209"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"424"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997424-209" id="mwBaY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBac"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBag">[</span>202<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBak">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt795" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHastings2006369_210-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHastings2006369"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHastings2006369-210"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hastings"},"2":{"wt":"2006"},"p":{"wt":"369"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHastings2006369-210" id="mwBao"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBas"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaw">[</span>203<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBa0">]</span></span></a></sup> Hitler relieved Kluge of his command of OB West on 15 August and replaced him with Field Marshal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Model" title="Walter Model" id="mwBa4">Walter Model</a>. Kluge committed suicide on 19 August after Hitler became aware of his involvement in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/20_July_plot" title="20 July plot" id="mwBa8">20 July plot</a>.<sup about="#mwt798" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997421,_444_211-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997421, 444"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997421,_444-211"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"421, 444"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997421,_444-211" id="mwBbA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBbE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbI">[</span>204<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt801" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEvans2008642_212-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEvans2008642"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEvans2008642-212"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Evans"},"2":{"wt":"2008"},"p":{"wt":"642"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEvans2008642-212" id="mwBbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBbU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbY">[</span>205<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbc">]</span></span></a></sup> An invasion in southern France (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dragoon" title="Operation Dragoon" id="mwBbg">Operation Dragoon</a>) was launched on 15 August.<sup about="#mwt804" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009445,_447_213-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009445, 447"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009445,_447-213"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"445, 447"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009445,_447-213" id="mwBbk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBbo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbs">[</span>206<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBb0">The French Resistance in Paris rose against the Germans on 19 August.<sup about="#mwt807" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997429_214-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997429"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997429-214"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"429"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997429-214" id="mwBb4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBb8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcA">[</span>207<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcE">]</span></span></a></sup> Eisenhower initially wanted to bypass the city to pursue other targets, but amid reports that the citizens were going hungry and Hitler's stated intention to destroy it, de Gaulle insisted that it should be taken immediately.<sup about="#mwt810" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009481,_483,_494_215-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009481, 483, 494"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009481,_483,_494-215"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"481, 483, 494"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009481,_483,_494-215" id="mwBcI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBcM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcQ">[</span>208<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcU">]</span></span></a></sup> French forces of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Armored_Division_(France)" title="2nd Armored Division (France)" id="mwBcY">2nd Armoured Division</a> under General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_Leclerc_de_Hauteclocque" title="Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque" id="mwBcc">Philippe Leclerc</a> arrived from the west on 24 August, while the U.S. 4th Infantry Division pressed up from the south. Scattered fighting continued throughout the night, and by the morning of 25 August <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_of_Paris" title="Liberation of Paris" id="mwBcg">Paris was liberated</a>.<sup about="#mwt813" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997430_216-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997430"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997430-216"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"430"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997430-216" id="mwBck"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBco"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcs">[</span>209<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBc0">Operations continued in the British and Canadian sectors until the end of the month. On 25 August, the U.S. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Armored_Division_(United_States)" title="2nd Armored Division (United States)" id="mwBc4">2nd Armored Division</a> fought its way into <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elbeuf" title="Elbeuf" id="mwBc8">Elbeuf</a>, making contact with British and Canadian armoured divisions.<sup about="#mwt816" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286_217-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStacey1960286"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286-217"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stacey"},"2":{"wt":"1960"},"p":{"wt":"286"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286-217" id="mwBdA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBdE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdI">[</span>210<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdM">]</span></span></a></sup> The 2nd Canadian Infantry Division advanced into the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forêt_de_Rouvray" title="Forêt de Rouvray" id="mwBdQ">Forêt de la Londe</a> on the morning of 27 August. The area was strongly held; the 4th and 6th Canadian brigades suffered many casualties over the course of three days as the Germans fought a delaying action in terrain well suited to defence. The Germans pulled back on 29 August, withdrawing over the Seine the next day.<sup about="#mwt819" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286_217-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStacey1960286"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286-217"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stacey"},"2":{"wt":"1960"},"p":{"wt":"286"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960286-217" id="mwBdU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBdY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdc">[</span>210<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdg">]</span></span></a></sup> On the afternoon of 30 August, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3rd_Canadian_Division" title="3rd Canadian Division" id="mwBdk">3rd Canadian Infantry Division</a> crossed the Seine near Elbeuf and entered <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rouen" title="Rouen" id="mwBdo">Rouen</a> to a jubilant welcome.<sup about="#mwt822" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStacey1948219_218-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStacey1948219"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1948219-218"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stacey"},"2":{"wt":"1948"},"p":{"wt":"219"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1948219-218" id="mwBds"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBdw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBd0">[</span>211<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBd4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="16" id="mwBd8" aria-labelledby="Campaign_close"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Campaign_close">Campaign close</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16" title="Edit section: Campaign close"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBeA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBeE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg/330px-Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg/960px-Canadiannaziflag1944.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2480" data-file-height="3472" data-file-type="bitmap" height="392" width="280" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.1" id="mwBeI"/></a><figcaption id="mwBeM">Canadian soldiers with a captured <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_Germany#Nazi_Germany" title="Flag of Germany" id="mwBeQ">Nazi flag</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBeU">Eisenhower took direct command of all Allied ground forces on 1 September. Concerned about German counter-attacks and the limited materiel arriving in France, he decided to continue operations on a broad front rather than attempting narrow thrusts.<sup about="#mwt825" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342_219-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342-219"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"341–342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342-219" id="mwBeY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBec"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeg">[</span>212<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBek">]</span></span></a></sup> The linkup of the Normandy forces with the Allied forces in southern France occurred on 12 September as part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drive_to_the_Siegfried_Line" title="Drive to the Siegfried Line" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBeo">drive to the Siegfried Line</a>.<sup about="#mwt828" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997485_220-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997485"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997485-220"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"485"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997485-220" id="mwBes"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBew"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBe0">[</span>213<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBe4">]</span></span></a></sup> On 17 September, Montgomery launched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Market_Garden" title="Operation Market Garden" id="mwBe8">Operation Market Garden</a>, an unsuccessful attempt by Anglo-American airborne troops to capture bridges in the Netherlands to allow ground forces to cross the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhine" title="Rhine" id="mwBfA">Rhine</a> into Germany.<sup about="#mwt831" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342_219-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342-219"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"341–342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009341–342-219" id="mwBfE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBfI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfM">[</span>212<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The Allied advance slowed due to German resistance and the lack of supplies (especially fuel). On 16 December the Germans launched the Ardennes Offensive, also known as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Bulge" title="Battle of the Bulge" id="mwBfU">Battle of the Bulge</a>, the last major German offensive of the war on the Western Front. A series of successful Soviet actions began with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vistula–Oder_Offensive" title="Vistula–Oder Offensive" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBfY">Vistula–Oder Offensive</a> on 12 January. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Adolf_Hitler" title="Death of Adolf Hitler" id="mwBfc">Hitler committed suicide</a> on 30 April as Soviet troops neared his <i id="mwBfg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Führerbunker" title="Führerbunker" id="mwBfk">Führerbunker</a></i> in Berlin, and Germany surrendered on 7 May 1945.<sup about="#mwt834" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109_221-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109-221"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"109"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109-221" id="mwBfo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBfs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfw">[</span>214<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBf0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBf4">The Normandy landings were the largest seaborne invasion in history, with nearly 5,000 landing and assault craft, 289 escort vessels, and 277 minesweepers.<sup about="#mwt837" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974_140-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor200974"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974-140"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"74"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor200974-140" id="mwBf8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgE">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgI">]</span></span></a></sup> The opening of another front in western Europe was a tremendous psychological blow for Germany's military, who feared a repetition of the two-front war of World War I. The Normandy landings also heralded the start of the "race for Europe" between the Soviet forces and the Western powers, which some historians consider to be the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Origins_of_the_Cold_War" title="Origins of the Cold War" id="mwBgM">start of the Cold War</a>.<sup about="#mwt840" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGaddis1990149_222-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGaddis1990149"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGaddis1990149-222"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gaddis"},"2":{"wt":"1990"},"p":{"wt":"149"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGaddis1990149-222" id="mwBgQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgY">[</span>215<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBgg">Victory in Normandy stemmed from several factors. German preparations along the Atlantic Wall were only partially finished; shortly before D-Day Rommel reported that construction was only 18 per cent complete in some areas as resources were diverted elsewhere.<sup about="#mwt843" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997290_223-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997290"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997290-223"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"290"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997290-223" id="mwBgk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgs">[</span>216<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgw">]</span></span></a></sup> The deceptions undertaken in Operation Fortitude were successful, leaving the Germans obliged to defend a huge stretch of coastline.<sup about="#mwt846" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009343_224-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009343"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009343-224"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"343"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009343-224" id="mwBg0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBg4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBg8">[</span>217<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Allies achieved and maintained air superiority, which meant that the Germans were unable to make observations of the preparations underway in Britain and were unable to interfere via bomber attacks.<sup about="#mwt849" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997289_225-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997289"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997289-225"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"289"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997289-225" id="mwBhE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBhI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhM">[</span>218<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt852" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEUSAF_Reports1957531–535_226-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEUSAF Reports1957531–535"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEUSAF_Reports1957531–535-226"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"USAF Reports"},"2":{"wt":"1957"},"pp":{"wt":"531–535"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEUSAF_Reports1957531–535-226" id="mwBhU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBhY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhc">[</span>219<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhg">]</span></span></a></sup> Transport infrastructure in France was severely disrupted by Allied bombers and the French Resistance, making it difficult for the Germans to bring up reinforcements and supplies.<sup about="#mwt855" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200936_227-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200936"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200936-227"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"36"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga200936-227" id="mwBhk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBho"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhs">[</span>220<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhw">]</span></span></a></sup> Much of the opening artillery barrage was off-target or not concentrated enough to have any impact,<sup about="#mwt858" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECopp2003259_228-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECopp2003259"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp2003259-228"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Copp"},"2":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"259"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp2003259-228" id="mwBh0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBh4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBh8">[</span>221<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiA">]</span></span></a></sup> but the specialised armour worked well except on Omaha, providing close artillery support for the troops as they disembarked onto the beaches.<sup about="#mwt861" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997291_229-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997291"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997291-229"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"291"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997291-229" id="mwBiE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBiI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiM">[</span>222<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The indecisiveness and overly complicated command structure of the German high command was also a factor in the Allied success.<sup about="#mwt864" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997292_230-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997292"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997292-230"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"292"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997292-230" id="mwBiU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBiY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBic">[</span>223<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBig">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="17" id="mwBik" aria-labelledby="Casualties"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Casualties">Casualties</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=17" title="Edit section: Casualties"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<section data-mw-section-id="18" id="mwBio" aria-labelledby="Allies"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Allies">Allies</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=18" title="Edit section: Allies"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBis"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers,_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBiw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers,_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers%2C_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg/250px-Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers%2C_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers%2C_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg/500px-Omaha_Beach_wounded_soldiers%2C_1944-06-06_P012901.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3201" data-file-height="2480" data-file-type="bitmap" height="194" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBi0"/></a><figcaption id="mwBi4">American assault troops injured while storming Omaha Beach</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBi8">From D-Day to 21 August, the Allies landed 2,052,299 men in northern France. The cost of the Normandy campaign was high for both sides.<sup about="#mwt867" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341_25-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"341"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25" id="mwBjA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBjE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjI">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjM">]</span></span></a></sup> Between 6 June and the end of August, the American armies suffered 124,394 casualties, of whom 20,668 were killed,<sup about="#mwt870" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-American_casualties_21-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"American casualties","group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"html":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"name":{"wt":"American casualties"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-American_casualties-21" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBjQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBjU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjY">[</span>c<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjc">]</span></span></a></sup> and 10,128 were missing.<sup about="#mwt873" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341_25-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"341"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25" id="mwBjg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBjk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjo">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBjs">]</span></span></a></sup> Casualties within the First Canadian and Second British Armies are placed at 83,045: 15,995 killed, 57,996 wounded, and 9,054 missing.<sup about="#mwt876" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-232" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-232"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"British casualties are sourced from \"War Diary, 21st Army Group, &apos;A&apos; Section, SITEP\" dated 29 August 1944.{{sfn|D&apos;Este|2004|pp=517–518}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-232" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBjw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBj0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBj4">[</span>h<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBj8">]</span></span></a></sup> Of these, Canadian losses amounted to 18,444, with 5,021 killed in action.<sup about="#mwt882" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStacey1960271_23-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStacey1960271"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960271-23"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stacey"},"2":{"wt":"1960"},"p":{"wt":"271"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStacey1960271-23" id="mwBkA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkI">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkM">]</span></span></a></sup> One in seven Canadian soldiers killed between 6–11 June were killed after surrendering, in a series of executions that would be named the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_Massacres" title="Normandy Massacres" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBkQ">Normandy Massacres</a>.<sup about="#mwt885" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMargolian1998125–155_233-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMargolian1998125–155"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMargolian1998125–155-233"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Margolian"},"2":{"wt":"1998"},"pp":{"wt":"125–155"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMargolian1998125–155-233" id="mwBkU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkc">[</span>225<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkg">]</span></span></a></sup> The Allied air forces, having flown 480,317 sorties in support of the invasion, lost 4,101 aircraft and 16,714 airmen (8,536 members of the USAAF, and 8,178 flying under the command of the RAF).<sup about="#mwt888" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341_25-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"341"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341-25" id="mwBkk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBko"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBks">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt891" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004487–488_234-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004487–488"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004487–488-234"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ellis"},"2":{"wt":"Allen"},"3":{"wt":"Warhurst"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"487–488"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004487–488-234" id="mwBk0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBk4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBk8">[</span>226<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Free French SAS paratroopers suffered 77 killed, with 197 wounded and missing.<sup about="#mwt894" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECorta1997288–289_235-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECorta1997288–289"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta1997288–289-235"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Corta"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"pp":{"wt":"288–289"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECorta1997288–289-235" id="mwBlE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlM">[</span>227<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Allied tank losses have been estimated at 4,000, with losses split evenly between the American and British/Canadian armies.<sup about="#mwt897" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342_27-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342-27"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342-27" id="mwBlU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlc">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlg">]</span></span></a></sup> Historians slightly differ on overall casualties during the campaign, with the lowest losses totaling 225,606<sup about="#mwt900" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009522_236-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009522"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009522-236"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"522"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009522-236" id="mwBlk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBls">[</span>228<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt903" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTED'Este2004517_237-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTED&apos;Este2004517"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTED&apos;Este2004517-237"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"D&apos;Este"},"2":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"517"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTED'Este2004517-237" id="mwBl0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBl4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBl8">[</span>229<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmA">]</span></span></a></sup> and the highest at 226,386.<sup about="#mwt906" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004488,_493_238-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004488, 493"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004488,_493-238"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ellis"},"2":{"wt":"Allen"},"3":{"wt":"Warhurst"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"pp":{"wt":"488, 493"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEllisAllenWarhurst2004488,_493-238" id="mwBmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmM">[</span>230<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt909" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341–342_239-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341–342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341–342-239"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"pp":{"wt":"341–342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003341–342-239" id="mwBmU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBmY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmc">[</span>231<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="19" id="mwBmk" aria-labelledby="Germany"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Germany">Germany</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=19" title="Edit section: Germany"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBmo"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBms"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg/500px-St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg/960px-St.-Lambert-surrender.3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2190" data-file-height="1638" data-file-type="bitmap" height="262" width="350" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.4" id="mwBmw"/></a><figcaption id="mwBm0">German forces surrender in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Lambert-sur-Dive" title="Saint-Lambert-sur-Dive" id="mwBm4">Saint-Lambert-sur-Dive</a>, 21 August 1944 </figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBm8">Allied forces in northern France reported the capture of 47,000 Germans in June, 36,000 in July, and 150,000 in August, a total of 233,000 for the three months of Overlord.<sup about="#mwt915" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-240" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-240"}}'><a href="#cite_note-240" id="mwBnA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBnE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnI">[</span>232<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnM">]</span></span></a></sup> Around 80,000 German soldiers are buried in Normandy, although this figure includes an unreported number of Germans who died before the battle or in captivity after the end of the fighting.<sup about="#mwt912" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEInmanStaines2019La_Cambe_German_Military_Cemetery_28-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEInmanStaines2019La Cambe German Military Cemetery"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEInmanStaines2019La_Cambe_German_Military_Cemetery-28"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Inman"},"2":{"wt":"Staines"},"3":{"wt":"2019"},"loc":{"wt":"La Cambe German Military Cemetery"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEInmanStaines2019La_Cambe_German_Military_Cemetery-28" id="mwBnQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBnU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnY">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBng">German forces in France reported losses of 158,930 men between D-Day and 14 August, just before the start of Operation Dragoon in Southern France.<sup about="#mwt916" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343_241-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343-241"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"343"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343-241" id="mwBnk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBno"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBns">[</span>233<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnw">]</span></span></a></sup> In action at the Falaise pocket, 50,000 men were lost, of whom 10,000 were killed and 40,000 captured.<sup about="#mwt919" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342_27-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342-27"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"342"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342-27" id="mwBn0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBn4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBn8">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoA">]</span></span></a></sup> Sources vary on the total German casualties. Niklas Zetterling notes that OB West's figures for summer 1944 in the west (including Operation Dragoon in southern France) amounted to 289,000: 23,019 killed, 67,060 wounded, and 198,616 missing. He states that the record is generally reliable, but also that it may have underestimated losses in some places, such as Cherbourg.<sup about="#mwt922" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZetterling200077_242-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZetterling200077"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling200077-242"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"p":{"wt":"77"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling200077-242" id="mwBoE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoM">[</span>234<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt925" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-244" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-244"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The German official history stated that Army Group G, which faced the Dragoon landings, suffered 141,000 casualties by mid-September. This figure is based on several internal reports and work conducted by German historians.{{sfn|Boog|Krebs|Vogel|2006|p=661}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-244" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBoU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoc">[</span>i<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBog">]</span></span></a></sup> Zetterling goes on to estimate German army casualties in the Normandy region specifically from June 6 to August as 210,000; however, he notes that "the Germans most likely suffered further manpower losses when air or naval bases were overrun. On this no figures have been available for this study."<sup about="#mwt931" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZetterling200074_245-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZetterling200074"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling200074-245"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"p":{"wt":"74"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling200074-245" id="mwBok"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBos">[</span>236<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBow">]</span></span></a></sup> Other sources arrive at higher estimates: 400,000 (200,000 killed or wounded and a further 200,000 captured),<sup about="#mwt934" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109_221-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109-221"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Whitmarsh"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"109"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWhitmarsh2009109-221" id="mwBo0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBo4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBo8">[</span>214<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpA">]</span></span></a></sup> 500,000 (290,000 killed or wounded, 210,000 captured),<sup about="#mwt937" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434_17-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434-17"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"434"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434-17" id="mwBpE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpM">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt940" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-247" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-247"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The German official history highlights Allied intelligence reports that indicated OB West lost 400,000 to 500,000 men{{sfn|Boog|Krebs|Vogel|2006|p=616}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-247" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBpU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpc">[</span>j<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpg">]</span></span></a></sup> to 530,000 in total.<sup about="#mwt946" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGiangrecoMoorePolmar2004252_248-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGiangrecoMoorePolmar2004252"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGiangrecoMoorePolmar2004252-248"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Giangreco"},"2":{"wt":"Moore"},"3":{"wt":"Polmar"},"4":{"wt":"2004"},"p":{"wt":"252"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGiangrecoMoorePolmar2004252-248" id="mwBpk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBps">[</span>238<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBp0">There are no exact figures regarding German tank losses in Normandy. Approximately 2,300 tanks and assault guns were committed to the battle,<sup about="#mwt949" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-249" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-249"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The most common tank/assault gun deployed at Normandy by the Germans was by far the Panzer IV, followed by the Panther (650) and Stug III (550). Also present were 120–130 Tiger Is, 20 Tiger IIs, and smaller numbers of other types, including Marder and Jagdpanther self-propelled anti-tank guns.{{sfn|Buckley|2006|pp=117–120}} "}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-249" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBp4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBp8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqA">[</span>k<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqE">]</span></span></a></sup> of which only 100 to 120 crossed the Seine at the end of the campaign.<sup about="#mwt955" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434_17-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434-17"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wilmot"},"2":{"wt":"1997"},"p":{"wt":"434"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilmot1997434-17" id="mwBqI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqQ">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqU">]</span></span></a></sup> While German forces reported only 481 tanks destroyed between D-day and 31 July,<sup about="#mwt958" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343_241-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343-241"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"343"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003343-241" id="mwBqY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqg">[</span>233<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqk">]</span></span></a></sup> research conducted by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operations_research" title="Operations research" id="mwBqo">No. 2 Operational Research Section</a> of 21st Army Group indicates that the Allies destroyed around 550 tanks in June and July<sup about="#mwt961" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEShulman2007166_250-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEShulman2007166"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEShulman2007166-250"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Shulman"},"2":{"wt":"2007"},"p":{"wt":"166"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEShulman2007166-250" id="mwBqs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBq0">[</span>239<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBq4">]</span></span></a></sup> and another 500 in August,<sup about="#mwt964" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECopp2000399–400_251-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECopp2000399–400"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp2000399–400-251"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Copp"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"pp":{"wt":"399–400"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECopp2000399–400-251" id="mwBq8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrE">[</span>240<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrI">]</span></span></a></sup> for a total of 1,050 tanks destroyed, including 100 destroyed by aircraft.<sup about="#mwt967" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZetterling2000408_11-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZetterling2000408"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling2000408-11"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"p":{"wt":"408"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZetterling2000408-11" id="mwBrM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrU">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrY">]</span></span></a></sup> Luftwaffe losses amounted to 2,127 aircraft.<sup about="#mwt970" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342–343_30-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342–343"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342–343-30"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tamelander"},"2":{"wt":"Zetterling"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"pp":{"wt":"342–343"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETamelanderZetterling2003342–343-30" id="mwBrc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrk">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBro">]</span></span></a></sup><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_needing_additional_references" about="#mwt973" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBrs" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"additional citations needed","href":"./Template:Additional_citations_needed"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"March 2025"}},"i":0}}]}'/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_needing_additional_references_from_March_2025" about="#mwt973"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact" style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt973" id="mwBrw"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title="Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span title="This claim needs additional references to reliable sources. (March 2025)">additional citation(s) needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> By the end of the Normandy campaign, 55 German divisions (42 infantry and 13 panzer) had been rendered combat-ineffective; seven of these were disbanded. By September, OB West had only 13 infantry divisions, 3 panzer divisions, and 2 panzer brigades rated as combat-effective.<sup about="#mwt974" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZaloga2015470_252-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZaloga2015470"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZaloga2015470-252"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"p":{"wt":"470"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZaloga2015470-252" id="mwBr0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBr4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBr8">[</span>241<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="20" id="mwBsE" aria-labelledby="Civilians_and_French_heritage_buildings"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Civilians_and_French_heritage_buildings">Civilians and French heritage buildings</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=20" title="Edit section: Civilians and French heritage buildings"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBsI"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe,_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBsM"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe,_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe%2C_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg/330px-The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe%2C_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe%2C_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg/960px-The_Allied_Campaign_in_North-west_Europe%2C_6_June_1944_-_7_May_1945_B6794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1300" data-file-height="814" data-file-type="bitmap" height="207" width="330" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.3" id="mwBsQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwBsU">A British soldier escorts an elderly lady in heavily bombed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caen" title="Caen" id="mwBsY">Caen</a>, July 1944</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBsc">During the liberation of Normandy, between 13,632 and 19,890 French civilians were killed,<sup about="#mwt977" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009336–337_33-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009336–337"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009336–337-33"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"336–337"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009336–337-33" id="mwBsg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBsk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBso">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBss">]</span></span></a></sup> and more were seriously wounded.<sup about="#mwt980" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519_32-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"519"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32" id="mwBsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBs0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBs4">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBs8">]</span></span></a></sup> In addition to those who died during the campaign, 11,000 to 19,000 Normans are estimated to have been killed during pre-invasion bombing.<sup about="#mwt983" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519_32-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"519"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32" id="mwBtA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtI">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtM">]</span></span></a></sup> A total of 70,000 French civilians were killed throughout the course of the war.<sup about="#mwt986" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519_32-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"519"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009519-32" id="mwBtQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtY">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtc">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_mine" title="Land mine" id="mwBtg">Land mines</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unexploded_ordnance" title="Unexploded ordnance" id="mwBtk">unexploded ordnance</a> continued to inflict casualties upon the Norman population following the end of the campaign.<sup about="#mwt989" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009305_253-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009305"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009305-253"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"305"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009305-253" id="mwBto"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBts"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtw">[</span>242<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBt0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBt4">Prior to the invasion, SHAEF issued instructions (later the basis for the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hague_Convention_for_the_Protection_of_Cultural_Property_in_the_Event_of_Armed_Conflict" title="Hague Convention for the Protection of Cultural Property in the Event of Armed Conflict" id="mwBt8">1954 Hague Convention Protocol I</a>) emphasising the need to limit the destruction to French heritage sites. These sites, named in the Official Civil Affairs Lists of Monuments, were not to be used by troops unless permission was received from the upper echelons of the chain of command.<sup about="#mwt992" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009350_254-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009350"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009350-254"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"350"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009350-254" id="mwBuA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBuE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuI">[</span>243<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuM">]</span></span></a></sup> Nevertheless, church spires and other stone buildings throughout the area were damaged or destroyed to prevent them being used by the Germans.<sup about="#mwt995" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520_255-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520-255"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"520"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520-255" id="mwBuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBuU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuY">[</span>244<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuc">]</span></span></a></sup> Efforts were made to prevent reconstruction workers from using rubble from important ruins to repair roads, and to search for artefacts.<sup about="#mwt998" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354_256-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009354"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"354"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256" id="mwBug"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBuk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuo">[</span>245<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBus">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayeux_Tapestry" title="Bayeux Tapestry" id="mwBuw">Bayeux Tapestry</a> and other important cultural treasures had been stored at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Château_de_Sourches" title="Château de Sourches" id="mwBu0">Château de Sourches</a> near Le Mans from the start of the war, and survived intact.<sup about="#mwt1001" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352_257-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009352"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352-257"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"352"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352-257" id="mwBu4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBu8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvA">[</span>246<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvE">]</span></span></a></sup> The occupying German forces also kept a list of protected buildings, but their intent was to keep the facilities in good condition for use as accommodation by German troops.<sup about="#mwt1004" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354_256-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009354"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"354"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256" id="mwBvI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvQ">[</span>245<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBvY">Many cities and towns in Normandy were totally devastated by the fighting and bombings. By the end of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Caen" title="Battle of Caen" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBvc">Battle of Caen</a> there remained only 8,000 liveable quarters for a population of over 60,000.<sup about="#mwt1007" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520_255-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520-255"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Beevor"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"520"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBeevor2009520-255" id="mwBvg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvo">[</span>244<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvs">]</span></span></a></sup> Of the 18 listed churches in Caen, four were seriously damaged and five were destroyed, along with 66 other listed monuments.<sup about="#mwt1010" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352_257-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009352"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352-257"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"352"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009352-257" id="mwBvw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBv0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBv4">[</span>246<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBv8">]</span></span></a></sup> In the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calvados_(department)" title="Calvados (department)" id="mwBwA">Calvados</a> department (location of the Normandy beachhead), 76,000 citizens were rendered homeless. Of Caen's 210 pre-war Jewish population, only one survived the war.<sup about="#mwt1013" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009337_258-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009337"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009337-258"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"337"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009337-258" id="mwBwE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBwI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwM">[</span>247<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBwU">Looting was perpetrated by all sides: the retreating Germans, the invading Allies, and the local French population.<sup about="#mwt1016" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354_256-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009354"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"354"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009354-256" id="mwBwY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBwc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwg">[</span>245<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwk">]</span></span></a></sup> Looting was  never condoned by Allied forces, and those who were found to be looting were punished.<sup about="#mwt1019" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFlint2009292_259-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFlint2009292"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009292-259"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Flint"},"2":{"wt":"2009"},"p":{"wt":"292"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFlint2009292-259" id="mwBwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBww">[</span>248<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBw0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="21" id="mwBw4" aria-labelledby="War_memorials_and_tourism"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="War_memorials_and_tourism">War memorials and tourism</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=21" title="Edit section: War memorials and tourism"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBw8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBxA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG/330px-Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG/960px-Beny-Sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery_-7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2032" data-file-height="1539" data-file-type="bitmap" height="227" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwBxE"/></a><figcaption id="mwBxI">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bény-sur-Mer_Canadian_War_Cemetery" title="Bény-sur-Mer Canadian War Cemetery" id="mwBxM">Bény-sur-Mer Canadian War Cemetery</a> </figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBxQ">The beaches of Normandy are still known by their invasion code names. Significant places have plaques, memorials, or small museums, and guide books and maps are available. Some of the German strong points remain preserved; Pointe du Hoc, in particular, is little changed from 1944. The remains of Mulberry harbour B still sits in the sea at Arromanches. Several <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_military_cemeteries_in_Normandy" title="List of military cemeteries in Normandy" id="mwBxU">large cemeteries in the area</a> serve as the final resting place for many of the Allied and German soldiers killed in the Normandy campaign.<sup about="#mwt1022" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009345–354_260-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009345–354"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009345–354-260"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ford"},"2":{"wt":"Zaloga"},"3":{"wt":"2009"},"pp":{"wt":"345–354"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFordZaloga2009345–354-260" id="mwBxY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxg">[</span>249<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBxo">Above the English Channel on a bluff at Omaha Beach, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normandy_American_Cemetery_and_Memorial" title="Normandy American Cemetery and Memorial" id="mwBxs">Normandy American Cemetery and Memorial</a> has hosted numerous visitors each year. The site covers <span about="#mwt1025" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBxw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"172.5"},"2":{"wt":"acres"},"3":{"wt":"ha"},"order":{"wt":"flip"}},"i":0}}]}'>69.8 hectares (172.5 acres)</span> and contains the remains of 9,388 American military dead, most of whom were killed during the invasion of Normandy and ensuing military operations in World War II. Included are graves of Army Air Corps crews shot down over France as early as 1942 and four American women.<sup about="#mwt1026" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTETraces_of_War_261-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTETraces of War"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTETraces_of_War-261"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Traces of War"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTETraces_of_War-261" id="mwBx0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBx4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBx8">[</span>250<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="22" id="mwByE" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Operation_Overlord&amp;action=edit&amp;section=22" title="Edit section: See also"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
